(210, 'I Almost Lost My Apple Account to the Most Sophisticated Phishing Attack I’ve Ever Experienced', 'How scammers weaponized Apple’s own support system to bypass Two Factor Authentication — and how I barely escaped', 'On Wednesday, I almost lost everything — my photos, my email, my entire digital life.\r\nA simple text message turned into the most sophisticated phishing attack I’ve ever seen. Here’s how modern scammers exploit even security-conscious users — and what I learned.\r\n\r\n3:17 PM: The Setup\r\nIt started with this text message:\r\n\r\n​I wasn’t trying to sign in. Someone else was trying to access my account.\r\n\r\nAt the same time, my iPhone, iPad, and Mac all lit up with verification notifications — the familiar pop-ups that appear when someone tries to sign into your Apple ID.\r\n\r\n​3:18 PM: Apple’s Automated Call\r\n​One minute later, my phone rang from a toll-free number. It was​ Apple’s automated system delivering another 2FA code by voice.\r\n\r\n​Between the text, the pop-ups, and the automated call, the message was clear: someone was trying to break in.\r\n\r\n3:21 PM: The First Scam Call\r\nThree minutes later, my phone rang — (404) 926–3085, an Atlanta number. The caller identified themselves as Apple Support.\r\n\r\n\"Your account is under attack. We’re opening a ticket to help you. Someone will contact you shortly.\"\r\n\r\nThe brief 28-second call ended.\r\n\r\n3:31 PM: The 25-Minute Con\r\n\r\nThe same number. This time, the caller was calm, professional, and kept me on the line for 25 minutes. They said they were following up on the attack. They’d already opened a support case to help secure my account. Here’s the clever part: They actually created a real Apple Support case in my name.​​\r\n\r\nThey had me go to my mailbox and walked me through the case confirmation email. Verifying that the email sender was legitimate. It checked out.\r\n\r\nThis gave them massive credibility. Apple’s own systems were sending me official emails confirming their case number.\r\n\r\nThe rep instructed me to reset my iCloud password. I hesitated. This felt wrong. I asked twice if my account was actually compromised. Both times, they said yes.\r\n\r\nWith all those verification codes and notifications from 3:17 PM, it seemed real. I reset my password.\r\n\r\nThe rep guided me through it. He never asked for my 2FA code, and let me complete the reset myself. This felt right.​\r\n\r\nThe Hook: How They Got Me\r\nThen came the pivot: “You should receive a text with a link to close your case”.\r\n\r\nSoon after, this text arrived:\r\n\r\nThe site looked like any other Apple page using https with a valid certificate.\r\n\r\nIt had a field for the case number. They asked me to enter it and, to reinforce my trust, read me the last 4 digits of the case which matched the ticket email confirmation. Everything seemed normal. The support page promptly displayed a list of steps, some completed, some in progress:\r\n\r\nI could see something like:\r\n✅ Ticket Opened\r\n✅ Account Frozen\r\n✅ Password Changed\r\n➡️ Ticket Closing\r\n\r\nThis was brilliant psychological manipulation. Each completed checkmark built trust. The “in progress” indicator created urgency. I was watching my account being “secured” in real-time — or so I thought. The rep said:\r\n\r\n\"You will now receive a confirmation code to close the ticket.\"\r\n\r\nImmediately after saying that, the page was replaced by the familiar 6-digit placeholder and I received an Apple Validation code by SMS. I entered it. This was the moment they won.\r\n\r\n3:47 PM: The Moment of Truth\r\nSeconds after entering that code, an email arrived which made my blood run cold.\r\n\r\nA device I didn’t own had signed into my account. This wasn’t legitimate.\r\n\r\nI told the rep. He said it was “expected as part of the security process.” I challenged the URL: appeal-apple.com wasn’t an Apple sub-domain.\r\n\r\nOut of extra caution, I immediately:\r\n1. Reset my password again (without their help)\r\n2. Told them I wasn’t comfortable continuing this call\r\n3. Said that I would prefer to let the ticket expire\r\n\r\nHe insisted this was “standard procedure.” I held firm.\r\n\r\nThe call dropped.\r\n\r\nI was left trembling in front of my devices, only then realizing that I had just escaped a massive catastrophe.\r\n\r\nWithin minutes, the Mac mini vanished from my device list. The phishing site redirected to Google. The Apple support ticket was closed.\r\n\r\nWhat Made This Attack Work\r\nThe Setup (3:17–3:18 PM): Multiple 2FA codes triggered to create panic\r\nThe Hook (3:21 PM): Brief call planting the seed of “help”\r\nThe Con (3:31 PM): Real Apple Support case (ID: 102750168703) created by attackers\r\nThe Trap (3:47 PM): “Confirmation code” that was actually a live 2FA code\r\n\r\nMy mistake: Typing in that final 2FA code on their phishing page.\r\nTheir downfall: The device sign-in email.\r\n\r\nThe Real Issue\r\nThe attackers exploited a critical flaw: Apple’s support system allows anyone to create legitimate support cases in others’ names.\r\nThis generates:\r\n• Real case numbers that verify on Apple’s website\r\n• Official emails from apple.com domains\r\n• Complete credibility using Apple’s own infrastructure\r\n\r\nLearnings\r\nDon\'t Trust\r\n• Unsolicited calls, even with legitimate case numbers\r\n• Links in texts/emails about security issues\r\n• Any request for a “confirmation code” during support calls\r\n• Calls that coincide with real security notifications\r\n\r\nAlways\r\n• Check that the site has a legitimate domain, regardless of certificate validity (appeal-apple.com ≠ apple.com)\r\n• Open tickets yourself, never trust a support ticket initiated by a third party, even by Apple\r\n• Watch for device sign-in notifications\r\n\r\nBest Defense\r\n• Use hardware security keys (YubiKey, Google Titan, etc.) for 2FA. Apple supports these starting iOS 16.3+ and macOS Ventura 13.2+ — they’re immune to phishing. Setup guide: https://support.apple.com/en-us/102637\r\n\r\nBottom line: Modern phishing uses corporate infrastructure against you. They don’t need spelling errors when they have real Apple case numbers. Stay skeptical, verify independently, and protect those 2FA codes like your digital life depends on it… because it does.\r\n\r\nHave you received suspicious Apple support calls?\r\n\r\nShare this post — this is an active attack.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 73, 1, 80, 0, NULL, 0, '2026-04-04 03:32:26', '2026-04-03 11:32:26', '2026-04-04 03:32:26', 'published', NULL, 0),
(211, 'Rumours of my Garden have been Greatly Exaggerated', 'My Three-Step Process to Enjoy Gardening Without a Garden', 'I have been writing about my backyard for over a year now (which sounds strange when I put that in words. I think my family still finds it a bit strange as well.) This would naturally lead you to expect that I spend an inordinate amount of time tending and caring for my property. In fact, several friends have let me know how much they would love to see my garden, assuming that I must have an impressive array of flowering plants and trees to show off based on how much I seem to have to say about it. Whenever this happens I am uncertain how to respond, because, well…I’m a terrible gardener.\r\n\r\nSure, I’ve tried my hand at working the soil. I’ve experimented with growing food to supplement our meals. I’ve dug out rocks from a garden bed to allow plant roots to grow unencumbered. I’ve shoveled truckloads of manure-rich earth dumped on my driveway to various sunny garden plots. While I may have learned a bit and enjoyed myself in these tasks, the results of my efforts have been less than impressive. (To be clear, any successful gardening you may see in my yard is entirely the work of my wife.)\r\n\r\nWhat I have become quite proficient at, through years of significant and steadfast practice, is letting my yard go feral. This is no easy feat! The peer pressure from neighbours and society at large, much less the daggers occasionally stared at me by my wife, present real and challenging tests to my resolve to do nothing. I cannot say that I am always successful. Even today, my yard gets mowed two to three times a year, and, while we may no longer bag our leaves in the fall, they do get raked into more convenient locations on occasion. The struggle continues.\r\n\r\nI bring this up, because we are approaching the beginning of gardening season here in Canada (only two more months!). Fool’s Spring is behind us, and we have only to survive Spring of Deception, Third Winter and Mud Season before the ground thaws consistently. It is in this time of hope that many of us start salivating over thoughts of spending time outdoors in the extended sunlight coaxing life out of the ground with our dirt-encrusted hands. This is a special time. A time when our yards become the canvas upon which we paint our dreams of bright flowers and lush foliage. Even though I don’t really consider myself a gardener, I’m not immune to the seduction of a spring fling with a hose and a trowel. Who doesn’t want an excuse to spend all day outside when it is no longer jacket weather? The challenge, I’ve found, is figuring out how to spend my occasional forays into gardening in ways that are both satisfying and supportive of my “efforts” to leave my yard to natural reclamation. Here’s where I’ve landed.\r\n\r\nStep one, addition by subtraction. My least favourite part of gardening is taking multiple trips to a busy garden centre to buy stuff to put into my garden. Plants, seeds, soil, fertilizer, chemicals, gnomes, whimsical decorations…there is no end. Given that my goal is to allow my yard to approach a native landscape that attracts a diverse assortment of native species, it seems strange to source so many foreign goods. Solution: rather than putting things into my garden, I take things out!\r\n\r\nOne thing I have learned is that most yards are full of invasive plants and trees that have hitchhiked their way over from Europe and Asia. These greedy species will gladly take over huge swaths of our yards if we sit back and let them outcompete their native competition. To counter their advance, I like to use my spring gardening energy to rip buckthorn trees out of the ground and eliminate the garlic mustard polluting the soil with their allelopathic chemicals. It can feel like a thankless task at times, but with time and patience, it has been very satisfying to watch my yard transform from a reservoir of invaders to a safe haven oasis for native species. Just remember to take before and after pictures to remind yourself of all your hard work.\r\n\r\nStep two, feed your friends. There does come a time when I just need to plant things in the ground. Call it a compulsion. Maybe I’m itching to practice what I have been reading about over the winter, or I am simply finding nature’s pace of reclamation a tad too slow. When this urge takes over, it is easy to be tempted by the availability, convenience and catchy descriptions of the many hybrid flowers and vegetables that nursery growers are eager to sell us. Humans have spent many generations breeding plants that meet our desires for bright colours and engorged edible bits. There is much to be said about growing our own food mere steps from our kitchens, although my experience is that there is much frustration to be had trying to fend off the squirrels, bunnies and insects that also want to devour my harvest.\r\n\r\nRather than spending my limited gardening energy fighting off my non-human neighbours from eating my meager harvest, I try to focus on planting food to attract more non-human neighbours into my yard. Native plants and trees provide food and shelter to a diverse array of native fauna, whether it’s pollinating bees, leaf-loving caterpillars or caterpillar-loving birds. Sadly, finding native seeds to plant in my yard can be challenging, as what is found in most nurseries and garden centres are likely non-native hybrids. An amazing alternative is the Ottawa Wildflower Seed Library that provides free seeds that people can “check out” to grow in their own gardens. Once the plants have flowered and gone to seed, people can “return” some seeds to the library to make them available for other people. The only trick is that many of the plants around here have adapted to our cold winter and require winter sowing before they will germinate, so it’s necessary to plan ahead before spring fever takes over.\r\n\r\nStep three, look, don’t touch. In my experience, the spring rush of gardening energy is soon replaced by the summer vibe of vacation and relaxation. This means that the garden of my spring dreams is usually killed off by a lack of watering, weeding and pest control. Another one of the benefits of native wildflower gardens is that they need less maintenance, being adapted to local conditions.\r\n\r\nWhile I may not have the energy to get my hands dirty and keep everything in check, I do still want an excuse to spend time outside in my yard during the summer months. This is when gardening is replaced by observation. When we take the time to slow down and look around, it is incredible how much is going on right under our noses. Buds are exploding open into leaves, flowers and fruit. Larvae of all descriptions are munching on plants and then getting munched on themselves by birds and wasps. Mushrooms are popping out of most anything dead or rotting to spread their spores. Whether you look at the big picture or the microscopic scale, nature is working its magic all around us and doesn’t charge admission to enjoy the show.\r\n\r\nIt is when I fell into observing nature in my backyard that I realized how much there was to write about. When combined with puttering, I find that observing nature can be just as satisfying and productive as gardening without all the frustration that comes with going to the store, getting your clothes all muddy and dealing with the inevitable disappointment of another failed harvest. I like to observe nature in parks, forests and on vacation as well, but it’s always nice to avoid the commute and experience nature right outside your home. After all, it’s already there waiting to be seen. All you have to do is look.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 73, 6, 80, 0, NULL, 0, '2026-04-04 03:39:44', '2026-04-03 11:39:44', '2026-04-04 03:39:44', 'published', NULL, 0),
(212, 'A Deeper Dive Into the Iran War.', 'A Deeper Dive Into the Iran War.', 'According to the New York Times, the United States has deployed over 50,000 troops as “reinforcements” as the war in Iran continues to escalate. Thirteen U.S. soldiers have been killed and over 300 injured, according to Al Jazeera. Reports from the Washington Post claim civilian deaths in Iran have surpassed 1,500, including 217 children.\r\n\r\nThe reaction and military strategy appear to mirror Israel’s patterns in Gaza, where the issue of proportionality has been widely debated. Proportionality is a concept in international humanitarian law and the Geneva Conventions, which states that military force should not be excessive in relation to the military objective being pursued, particularly when civilian casualties are expected. For example, the October 7 Hamas attacks killed at least 828 Israeli civilians. In the period following those attacks, Israeli military operations resulted in tens of thousands of Palestinian deaths, with some estimates exceeding 75,000. Whether this constitutes proportional force has been a major point of international debate, and similar questions are now being raised regarding the war in Iran.\r\n\r\nThis issue of proportionality represents a recurring flaw in Western strategic thinking. The assumption that overwhelming force will subdue an enemy often ignores the perspective of those whose lives are directly at risk and who may feel they have nothing left to lose. In many cases, overwhelming force does not eliminate resistance, but instead hardens it and increases long-term hostility.\r\n\r\nHistorically, air campaigns alone have rarely succeeded in subduing a determined enemy. In Vietnam, extensive U.S. bombing campaigns did not break North Vietnamese resistance. In Afghanistan, two decades of air strikes did not eliminate the Taliban. In Iraq, air superiority did not prevent insurgency. More recently, bombing campaigns in Gaza and Yemen have not eliminated militant groups. Instead, air campaigns often embolden resistance movements, increase recruitment for militant organizations, and raise domestic morale against what is perceived as a foreign aggressor. This historical pattern is important when analyzing the current strategy in Iran.\r\n\r\nFrom the perspective of the United States and Israel, however, the strategy does have clear military and political objectives. Air strikes are intended to destroy Iranian missile sites, military infrastructure, and command centers in order to reduce Iran’s ability to retaliate. Another major objective is protecting international shipping lanes, particularly oil routes in the Persian Gulf. There is also significant domestic political pressure in both the United States and Israel to respond forcefully to Iranian actions, which often pushes governments toward more aggressive military responses even when long-term outcomes are uncertain.\r\n\r\nMeanwhile, the closing of the Strait of Hormuz, along with attacks on Kharg Island and other oil infrastructure in the Gulf region, has created significant strain on global energy markets and international shipping routes. The Strait of Hormuz is one of the most important oil chokepoints in the world, with roughly a fifth of global oil shipments passing through it. Any disruption there affects oil prices, shipping insurance costs, global inflation, and supply chains across Europe, Asia, and the United States. Because of this, the conflict with Iran is not just a regional war, but one with major global economic consequences.\r\n\r\nAs seen above, on March 31st, traffic in the Strait of Hormuz is slowly resuming. This comes after guarantees from the Iranian government that certain countries would be allowed to pass through the Strait safely. The partial reopening suggests Iran is attempting to use the strait not only as a military pressure point, but also as political and economic leverage, allowing some oil shipments to pass while still demonstrating that it has the ability to disrupt global trade at any time.\r\n\r\nEven temporary disruptions in the Strait of Hormuz have major global consequences. A significant portion of the world’s oil supply passes through this narrow waterway, meaning any threat to shipping immediately affects oil prices, shipping insurance costs, and global markets. Because of this, control over the Strait of Hormuz is one of Iran’s most powerful strategic tools, and it is one of the main reasons the United States maintains a heavy naval presence in the region.\r\n\r\nThe situation in the Strait shows that this war is not confined to Iran or Israel alone, but is instead affecting global trade, energy markets, and international shipping routes, pulling much of the world into the economic consequences of the conflict, even if they are not directly involved militarily.\r\n\r\nAs more satellite images are released to researchers — due to roughly a one-month delay in publicly available satellite imagery — more strikes will be analyzed in detail, including whether any of the documented attacks could amount to possible war crimes. Early in the conflict, Iranian strikes on Israel and the Gulf states were relatively well documented through open-source intelligence. This changed as strict censorship laws were implemented across many of the countries involved, and even to some extent in the United States, making open-source intelligence increasingly difficult and obscuring what is actually happening on the ground.\r\n\r\nThese retaliatory strikes from Iran have escalated significantly, with desalination plants on both sides of the conflict now being targeted. For the Gulf states, this poses a unique and extremely dangerous threat. Without functioning desalination plants, millions of people could be left without access to fresh water. In much of the Gulf region, desalination is not simply supplementary infrastructure but the primary source of drinking water. Countries such as Saudi Arabia, the United Arab Emirates, Qatar, and Bahrain rely heavily on desalination for the majority of their freshwater supply, meaning damage to these facilities does not just disrupt infrastructure but directly threatens civilian access to water. Unlike oil facilities or even power plants, desalination plants are directly tied to basic human survival, and prolonged disruption could quickly create a humanitarian crisis affecting millions of civilians. Because of this, attacks on desalination infrastructure are particularly controversial under international humanitarian law, which restricts attacks on infrastructure essential to civilian survival such as water supplies, food systems, and medical facilities.\r\n\r\nYet a threat closer to Americans is also looming. Since the start of the war, Donald Trump has not ruled out “boots on the ground.” According to multiple reports, Russia had been sharing satellite intelligence on U.S. bases and troop positions with Iran prior to the war. While this is not entirely surprising given the military and economic ties between Russia and Iran, it significantly increases the risk to U.S. forces in the region and raises the possibility of direct attacks on American bases or personnel as the war continues.\r\n\r\nAccording to leaked Pentagon planning scenarios, multiple possible paths forward in the war involve limited ground operations rather than large-scale invasions similar to the Iraq War. When paired with repeated statements from Trump and the continued targeting of Kharg Island — which handles the vast majority of Iran’s oil exports — it appears likely that any ground operations would focus on oil facilities, energy infrastructure, and military targets rather than full regime change or long-term occupation.\r\n\r\nThis approach would be consistent with the pattern seen so far in Trump’s second term, which has prioritized projection of power, economic pressure, and strategic strikes rather than large-scale nation-building or prolonged military occupations. However, Trump has also remained politically defensive about the war, particularly as American public opinion appears to be turning against it. According to the Pew Research Center, roughly six in ten Americans disapprove of the war, which could significantly influence how the conflict develops moving forward.\r\n\r\nWhat this has caused, in turn, is President Donald Trump to begin pushing back on some of his earlier war-hawkish rhetoric, instead shifting toward a public message focused more on negotiation and peace. This apparent shift in messaging may not necessarily reflect a change in military strategy, but rather a change in political strategy and public messaging.\r\n\r\nThis juxtaposition in Trump’s rhetoric appears to serve several purposes. First, it may buy time for additional troops and equipment to be deployed to the region while public attention shifts toward negotiations rather than escalation. Second, it may help relieve growing American public discontent with the war by presenting the administration as seeking peace rather than prolonged conflict. Third, it helps maintain support from Trump’s anti-war political base, which has historically been skeptical of foreign military interventions and long wars. Finally, mixed messaging and uncertainty may also serve a strategic purpose by making it more difficult for the Iranian leadership to predict the United States’ next move.\r\n\r\nUltimately, this suggests that Trump’s long-term objective may not be full regime change or large-scale military occupation, but rather a negotiated settlement that weakens Iran economically and strategically, particularly in relation to oil production, sanctions, and regional influence. From Iran’s perspective, however, the primary objective of the regime is survival. If the Iranian government remains in power and maintains control internally despite military and economic pressure, it could claim victory regardless of battlefield losses.\r\n\r\nIf this analysis proves correct, the war may not end with a clear military victory for either side, but rather with a negotiated settlement in which both sides claim success. In that scenario, the real losers of the war will likely be civilians, both in Iran and across the region, whose lives, infrastructure, and economies will be permanently affected by the conflict.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 73, 4, 80, 0, NULL, 0, '2026-04-04 03:42:20', '2026-04-03 11:42:20', '2026-04-04 03:42:20', 'published', NULL, 0),
(213, 'Did You Know… Roller Skating Has One of the Wildest Histories in Sports?', 'From mirror crashes to marathon derby races — your favorite hobby has been unhinged since day one.', 'Spring is creeping in and my quads are already calling my name.\r\n\r\nThere’s something about that first warm skate of the season that feels like a reset — wheels under your feet, wind doing its thing, the world suddenly fading into the rush and sound of wheels.\r\n\r\nI’ve also been invited to join roller derby.\r\n\r\nAgain.\r\n\r\nThis is not the first time.\r\n\r\nSo naturally, I went down a rabbit hole. And what I found? Roller skating history is unhinged in the best way.\r\n\r\nLet’s get into it.\r\n\r\nThe First Person to Skate in Public Crashed Into a Mirror\r\nNo, really.\r\n\r\nIn 1760, a Belgian inventor named John Joseph Merlin decided to debut his new roller skate invention at a fancy masquerade party in London. He rolled out onto the ballroom floor — while simultaneously playing the violin — with zero ability to stop or turn.\r\n\r\nHe crashed directly into a large mirror. Shattered it completely.\r\n\r\nThat’s it. That’s the origin story. No brakes, no steering, full send. And honestly? That chaotic, *I’ll figure it out when I get there* energy has defined skating culture ever since.\r\n\r\nPlot Twist: Inlines Are Actually Older Than Quads\r\nThis one surprised me.\r\n\r\nMost of us assume quad skates — the classic four-wheel, side-by-side setup — came first.\r\n\r\nThey feel more foundational somehow.\r\n\r\nBut inline skates are actually the older design. A Frenchman named Charles-Louis Petibled patented a three-wheel-in-a-line skate all the way back in 1819.\r\n\r\nThe problem? They couldn’t turn. At all. You just went straight and hoped for the best.\r\n\r\nIt wasn’t until 1863 that James Plimpton invented the quad with its rocking truck system — and that’s what finally let skaters actually steer.\r\n\r\nPlimpton opened the first public skating rink in New York City shortly after, and the rest is history.\r\n\r\nSo if you’re a quad girlie: your skates are the younger sibling.\r\n\r\nThe one who actually figured life out, but still — younger.\r\n\r\nThe 70s Were the Peak, the 90s Almost Ended It All\r\nQuad skating hit its cultural peak in the 1970s. Disco rinks were the place to be.\r\n\r\nEverybody skated.\r\n\r\nIt was social, it was musical, it was an event.\r\n\r\nThen the 90s happened.\r\n\r\nRollerblades took over almost overnight. The inline skate boom was massive — aggressive street skating, hockey, speed — and most quad skaters made the switch.\r\n\r\nRinks across the country closed.\r\n\r\nQuad culture nearly disappeared entirely.\r\n\r\nThe revival we’re living in right now? It’s a reclamation.\r\n\r\nEspecially for women, for BIPOC skaters, for queer communities who kept rink culture alive when everyone else moved on.\r\n\r\nThe return of quads isn’t just a trend — it’s people taking back something that was always theirs.\r\n\r\nRoller Derby Started on a Restaurant Tablecloth\r\nI need you to get this — roller derby was sketched out on a napkin.\r\n\r\nIn 1935, a Chicago promoter named Leo Seltzer was trying to come up with a depression-era spectacle that people would actually pay to watch.\r\n\r\nHe sketched his concept at a restaurant: teams of skaters racing the equivalent of 3,000 miles — the distance from San Diego to New York City — around a banked track.\r\n\r\nIt wasn’t supposed to be a contact sport. It was an endurance marathon.\r\n\r\nA sportswriter basically told him to lean into the physicality. People liked the crashes.\r\n\r\nThey liked the drama.\r\n\r\nSo he did — and it sold out.\r\n\r\nModern derby has evolved into something genuinely technical and brutal.\r\n\r\nThere’s the jammer (the point scorer), the blockers (exactly what it sounds like), and the pivot (the defensive captain who can become the jammer mid-jam).\r\n\r\nSkaters have alter-ego names like Princess Lay-Ya Flat.\r\n\r\nIt’s athletic theater and I respect it deeply.\r\n\r\nQuads and Inlines Aren’t Just Different Skates — They’re Different Worlds\r\nThe mechanical difference is real: quads give you lateral stability, which is why they’re perfect for dance skating, rink flow, and derby footwork.\r\n\r\nInlines give you longitudinal speed, which is why they dominate hockey, park skating, and aggressive street.\r\n\r\nBut the cultural split goes deeper than mechanics.\r\n\r\nQuad culture has historically been more community-centered, more femme-forward, more rooted in music and rhythm.\r\n\r\nInline culture leaned harder into extreme sports and competition.\r\n\r\nNeither is better.\r\n\r\nThey just have completely different souls and “rolls” 😉\r\n\r\nMaybe This Is My Derby Spring\r\nI’ve been skating with quads for a while now, and every season I find something new in it — a new style, a new reason, a new sense of what my body can do on eight wheels.\r\n\r\nAnd the derby invitations keep coming.\r\n\r\nI think about Seltzer sketching out his wild idea on a tablecloth, Merlin rolling straight into a mirror with a violin in his hands, Plimpton building a skate that could finally turn.\r\n\r\nAll of these people just doing the thing — messy, unprepared, inspired.\r\n\r\nMaybe this is the spring I finally say yes.\r\n\r\nHere are the sources that back my quirky Did You Know…? article:\r\n\r\n[1] 11 Things You Might Not Know About Roller Skates — Mental Floss https://www.mentalfloss.com/article/52168/11-things-you-might-not-know-about-roller-skates\r\n\r\n[2] History of inline skates — Wikipedia https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/History_of_inline_skates\r\n\r\n[3] The Incredible History of Roller Skating | Skate World Center https://skateworld1.com/the-incredible-history-of-roller-skating/\r\n\r\n[4] Transitions: From Quads to Blading — Dogdays Magazine https://dogdaysmagazine.com/transitions-from-quads-to-blading/\r\n\r\n[5] Roller Derby | National Museum of R https://www.rollerskatingmuseum.org/roller-derby\r\n\r\n[6] Roller Derby for Everybody: A History and Culture of Inclusivity https://folklife.si.edu/magazine/history-and-culture-of-roller-derby\r\n\r\n[7] 13 Things You Didn’t Know About Roller Derby — BuzzFeed https://www.buzzfeed.com/miller64/13-things-you-didnt-know-about-roller-derby\r\n\r\n[8] Cultural or mechanical difference between quad vs inline? — Reddit https://www.reddit.com/r/Rollerskating/comments/10qf920/cultural_or_mechanical_difference_between_quad_vs/\r\n\r\n[9] Inline roller vs quad roller differences tips and champion’s story https://www.flaneurz.com/en/blog/culture-roller-4/inline-skating-and-quad-skating-the-testimony-of-a-world-roller-skating-champion-88\r\n\r\nAre you a quad skater, an inline skater, or somewhere in between? Drop your skate story in the comments — I genuinely want to know.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 73, 5, 80, 0, NULL, 0, '2026-04-04 03:47:38', '2026-04-03 11:47:38', '2026-04-04 03:47:38', 'published', NULL, 0);
