(220, '10-day ceasefire deal between Israel and Lebanon takes effect', 'BEIRUT – A 10-day ceasefire deal agreed by Lebanon and Israel took effect on April 16, as US President Donald Trump said he was trying to set up the first meeting between the leaders of the two countries.  The truce, which Mr Trump said would begin at midnight local time (5am in Singapore) in Lebanon and Israel, comes as Washington steps up efforts to reach a deal to end the war with Iran, with Tehran insisting a Lebanon truce must be part of any agreement.   Gunfire rang out in Beirut’s southern suburbs, Hezbollah’s heartland, as the ceasefire came into effect – an apparent bout of spontaneous celebration, although that could not be confirmed.  The Middle East war began when the US and Israel attacked Iran on Feb 28, and Lebanon was pulled in when Hezbollah fired rockets at Israel on March 2.  Since then, Israeli strikes in Lebanon have killed more than 2,000 people and displaced over one million, while Israeli ground forces have invaded the country’s south.   As the ceasefire came into force, Israel’s military said it had struck over 380 “Hezbollah terror organisation targets in southern Lebanon” and was on “high alert” to resume strikes.  Top stories Explore top stories from all sections in one place  Despite the Iran war breaking out at the end of February, March saw non-oil domestic exports growing for the seventh consecutive month. Business  Singapore key exports surge 15.3% in March on AI-driven electronics demand School bus operators are hoping for more support to help them cope with surging diesel fuel costs. Singapore  Higher fuel prices: MOE asks schools to consider bus fare hike requests for external activities Mdm Choi Soon-duk with her 93-year-old haenyeo mentor. The Jeju haenyeo community is known for its tight-knit and matrilineal structure. Asia  Coming up for air: Done with city life, midlifers dive into Jeju’s haenyeo trade Employees discuss a character from the AI-generated series \"Mahabharat\" seen on a laptop screen at Galleri5, the tech studio arm of Collective Artists Network, at its office in Bengaluru, India, November 26, 2025. Asia  The end of ‘easy’ IT jobs? India’s tech workforce faces AI challenges Netflix chief Reed Hastings will not stand for re-election at the streaming service\'s  annual meeting in June, and plans to focus on philanthropy and other pursuits. Business  Netflix co-founder Reed Hastings exits as streaming pioneer hunts for growth Investors have raised questions ahead of SBS Transit\'s AGM as the transport operator’s dominance in the bus segment has come under pressure. Business  Investors question SBS Transit’s competitiveness, board oversight ahead of AGM The US leader said the deal to halt hostilities came after “excellent” phone calls with Israeli Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu and Lebanese President Joseph Aoun.  “These two Leaders have agreed that in order to achieve PEACE between their Countries, they will formally begin a 10 Day CEASEFIRE at 5 P.M. EST,” Mr Trump said on his Truth Social network.   He later said he expected Mr Netanyahu and Mr Aoun to visit the White House “over the next four or five days”.  A top-level face-to-face meeting between the Lebanese and Israeli leadership would be a watershed moment for the region.  An Israeli hospital spokesman said three people were injured on April 16, shortly before the truce began.  An Israeli strike on the southern Lebanese town of Ghazieh killed at least seven people and wounded 33, Lebanon’s Health Ministry said earlier on Thursday.  Mr Netanyahu said the ceasefire with Lebanon offered an opportunity for a “historic peace agreement” with Beirut – but insisted the disarmament of the militant group Hezbollah remained a precondition.  Mr Trump said Hezbollah was included in the ceasefire, but according to the US State Department, the truce committed Lebanon itself to dismantle the Iran-backed militant group.  UN Secretary-General Antonio Guterres welcomed the ceasefire, and pointedly called on “all actors to fully respect” it – phrasing that would include Hezbollah.  ‘Very happy’ In Beirut, housewife Jamal Shehab, 61, applauded the truce.  “We are very happy that a ceasefire has been reached in Lebanon because we are tired of war, and we want safety and peace,” she said.  Sitting at a cafe in Beirut, lawyer Tarek Bou Khalil told AFP that “it’s well known Trump cannot be taken at his word, and Netanyahu cannot be trusted”.  “But we know that... the pressures of the war with Iran and the blunders of Netanyahu and the enemy army in south Lebanon, forced them into a ceasefire,” he added.  Mr Trump spoke to reporters about the ceasefire as he left the White House for a trip to Las Vegas.  “It’s very exciting,” Mr Trump said in response to a question from an AFP reporter. “Today they’re going to be having a ceasefire, and that’ll include Hezbollah.”  More on this topic Hopes for Middle East peace grow as Israel discusses Lebanon ceasefire In new war with Israel, Hezbollah defies notion that it was crippled Mr Trump later said Lebanon would “take care of Hezbollah”, adding that he believed the Tehran-backed group would stick to the ceasefire.  A Hezbollah lawmaker told AFP it would “cautiously adhere” to the ceasefire if Israel stopped attacks.  Mr Ibrahim al-Moussawi thanked Iran for having applied pressure in Lebanon’s favour – adding that “the ceasefire would not have happened without Iran considering the ceasefire as equal to closing the Strait of Hormuz”.  Mr Netanyahu said Israel agreed to the truce but will maintain a 10km “security zone” along the border in southern Lebanon. He added that Israel maintained two conditions for the ceasefire: Hezbollah’s disarmament, and a lasting peace agreement “based on strength”.  Iran deal ‘very close’ Lebanese Prime Minister Nawaf Salam welcomed Mr Trump’s announcement of a ceasefire, saying a truce was a “key Lebanese demand that we have pursued since the very first day of the war” between Hezbollah and Israel.  Ahead of the ceasefire, Mr Aoun’s office thanked Mr Trump for his “efforts” to secure the truce. But the Lebanese President rejected Mr Trump’s request for a direct call with Mr Netanyahu, an official source told AFP.  The ceasefire comes after the Israeli and Lebanese ambassadors met in Washington this week – the first meeting of its kind since 1993. That truce could also boost Mr Trump’s continuing efforts to reach a deal to end the war with Iran.  Mr Trump said Washington was “very close” to a peace deal with Iran after six weeks of war and might travel to Pakistan to sign any agreement. AFP', 'BEIRUT – A 10-day ceasefire deal agreed by Lebanon and Israel took effect on April 16, as US President Donald Trump said he was trying to set up the first meeting between the leaders of the two countries.\r\n\r\nThe truce, which Mr Trump said would begin at midnight local time (5am in Singapore) in Lebanon and Israel, comes as Washington steps up efforts to reach a deal to end the war with Iran, with Tehran insisting a Lebanon truce must be part of any agreement.\r\n\r\n\r\nGunfire rang out in Beirut’s southern suburbs, Hezbollah’s heartland, as the ceasefire came into effect – an apparent bout of spontaneous celebration, although that could not be confirmed.\r\n\r\nThe Middle East war began when the US and Israel attacked Iran on Feb 28, and Lebanon was pulled in when Hezbollah fired rockets at Israel on March 2.\r\n\r\nSince then, Israeli strikes in Lebanon have killed more than 2,000 people and displaced over one million, while Israeli ground forces have invaded the country’s south.\r\n\r\n\r\nAs the ceasefire came into force, Israel’s military said it had struck over 380 “Hezbollah terror organisation targets in southern Lebanon” and was on “high alert” to resume strikes.\r\n\r\nTop stories\r\nExplore top stories from all sections in one place\r\n\r\nDespite the Iran war breaking out at the end of February, March saw non-oil domestic exports growing for the seventh consecutive month.\r\nBusiness\r\n\r\nSingapore key exports surge 15.3% in March on AI-driven electronics demand\r\nSchool bus operators are hoping for more support to help them cope with surging diesel fuel costs.\r\nSingapore\r\n\r\nHigher fuel prices: MOE asks schools to consider bus fare hike requests for external activities\r\nMdm Choi Soon-duk with her 93-year-old haenyeo mentor. The Jeju haenyeo community is known for its tight-knit and matrilineal structure.\r\nAsia\r\n\r\nComing up for air: Done with city life, midlifers dive into Jeju’s haenyeo trade\r\nEmployees discuss a character from the AI-generated series \"Mahabharat\" seen on a laptop screen at Galleri5, the tech studio arm of Collective Artists Network, at its office in Bengaluru, India, November 26, 2025.\r\nAsia\r\n\r\nThe end of ‘easy’ IT jobs? India’s tech workforce faces AI challenges\r\nNetflix chief Reed Hastings will not stand for re-election at the streaming service\'s  annual meeting in June, and plans to focus on philanthropy and other pursuits.\r\nBusiness\r\n\r\nNetflix co-founder Reed Hastings exits as streaming pioneer hunts for growth\r\nInvestors have raised questions ahead of SBS Transit\'s AGM as the transport operator’s dominance in the bus segment has come under pressure.\r\nBusiness\r\n\r\nInvestors question SBS Transit’s competitiveness, board oversight ahead of AGM\r\nThe US leader said the deal to halt hostilities came after “excellent” phone calls with Israeli Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu and Lebanese President Joseph Aoun.\r\n\r\n“These two Leaders have agreed that in order to achieve PEACE between their Countries, they will formally begin a 10 Day CEASEFIRE at 5 P.M. EST,” Mr Trump said on his Truth Social network.\r\n\r\n\r\nHe later said he expected Mr Netanyahu and Mr Aoun to visit the White House “over the next four or five days”.\r\n\r\nA top-level face-to-face meeting between the Lebanese and Israeli leadership would be a watershed moment for the region.\r\n\r\nAn Israeli hospital spokesman said three people were injured on April 16, shortly before the truce began.\r\n\r\nAn Israeli strike on the southern Lebanese town of Ghazieh killed at least seven people and wounded 33, Lebanon’s Health Ministry said earlier on Thursday.\r\n\r\nMr Netanyahu said the ceasefire with Lebanon offered an opportunity for a “historic peace agreement” with Beirut – but insisted the disarmament of the militant group Hezbollah remained a precondition.\r\n\r\nMr Trump said Hezbollah was included in the ceasefire, but according to the US State Department, the truce committed Lebanon itself to dismantle the Iran-backed militant group.\r\n\r\nUN Secretary-General Antonio Guterres welcomed the ceasefire, and pointedly called on “all actors to fully respect” it – phrasing that would include Hezbollah.\r\n\r\n‘Very happy’\r\nIn Beirut, housewife Jamal Shehab, 61, applauded the truce.\r\n\r\n“We are very happy that a ceasefire has been reached in Lebanon because we are tired of war, and we want safety and peace,” she said.\r\n\r\nSitting at a cafe in Beirut, lawyer Tarek Bou Khalil told AFP that “it’s well known Trump cannot be taken at his word, and Netanyahu cannot be trusted”.\r\n\r\n“But we know that... the pressures of the war with Iran and the blunders of Netanyahu and the enemy army in south Lebanon, forced them into a ceasefire,” he added.\r\n\r\nMr Trump spoke to reporters about the ceasefire as he left the White House for a trip to Las Vegas.\r\n\r\n“It’s very exciting,” Mr Trump said in response to a question from an AFP reporter. “Today they’re going to be having a ceasefire, and that’ll include Hezbollah.”\r\n\r\nMore on this topic\r\nHopes for Middle East peace grow as Israel discusses Lebanon ceasefire\r\nIn new war with Israel, Hezbollah defies notion that it was crippled\r\nMr Trump later said Lebanon would “take care of Hezbollah”, adding that he believed the Tehran-backed group would stick to the ceasefire.\r\n\r\nA Hezbollah lawmaker told AFP it would “cautiously adhere” to the ceasefire if Israel stopped attacks.\r\n\r\nMr Ibrahim al-Moussawi thanked Iran for having applied pressure in Lebanon’s favour – adding that “the ceasefire would not have happened without Iran considering the ceasefire as equal to closing the Strait of Hormuz”.\r\n\r\nMr Netanyahu said Israel agreed to the truce but will maintain a 10km “security zone” along the border in southern Lebanon. He added that Israel maintained two conditions for the ceasefire: Hezbollah’s disarmament, and a lasting peace agreement “based on strength”.\r\n\r\nIran deal ‘very close’\r\nLebanese Prime Minister Nawaf Salam welcomed Mr Trump’s announcement of a ceasefire, saying a truce was a “key Lebanese demand that we have pursued since the very first day of the war” between Hezbollah and Israel.\r\n\r\nAhead of the ceasefire, Mr Aoun’s office thanked Mr Trump for his “efforts” to secure the truce. But the Lebanese President rejected Mr Trump’s request for a direct call with Mr Netanyahu, an official source told AFP.\r\n\r\nThe ceasefire comes after the Israeli and Lebanese ambassadors met in Washington this week – the first meeting of its kind since 1993. That truce could also boost Mr Trump’s continuing efforts to reach a deal to end the war with Iran.\r\n\r\nMr Trump said Washington was “very close” to a peace deal with Iran after six weeks of war and might travel to Pakistan to sign any agreement. AFP', NULL, '9385db6ff48b30d6b3220549ae107b6896860b001508c71a0695d2462d5c9451', '{\"fingerprint\":\"9385db6ff48b30d6b3220549ae107b6896860b001508c71a0695d2462d5c9451\",\"trust_score\":49,\"passed\":false,\"publish_decision\":\"unreliable\",\"summary\":\"Only one trusted source strongly supports the draft, so confidence remains limited. At least one core claim is contradicted by trusted coverage.\",\"verdict\":\"Unreliable. Do not publish. The draft contains unsupported, false, or insufficiently verified information.\",\"metrics\":{\"factual_accuracy\":44,\"source_quality\":80,\"bias_detection\":80,\"logical_consistency\":60,\"completeness\":40},\"rubric_metrics\":{\"factual_accuracy\":20,\"source_quality\":20,\"bias_detection\":8,\"logical_consistency\":6,\"completeness\":4},\"source_url\":\"\",\"source_label\":\"Compared against 5 trusted CNA/ST sources.\",\"misinformation_highlights\":[{\"line\":\"The Middle East war began when the US and Israel attacked Iran on Feb 28, and Lebanon was pulled in when Hezbollah fired rockets at Israel on March 2.\",\"reason\":\"This core causal/chronological claim is unsupported by the supplied CNA/ST sources and is contradicted by the available reporting which does not state the war began with a US/Israel attack on Iran on Feb 28.\",\"source\":null}],\"improvement_suggestions\":[\"Add an exact CNA or ST article URL in the Reference Link field so the checker has a strong evidence anchor.\",\"Tighten factual claims by adding dates, locations, names, and figures that directly match CNA or ST reporting.\",\"Add missing context such as who said what, when it happened, where it happened, and what was officially announced.\",\"Rewrite or attribute weakly supported claims such as: \\\"Gunfire rang out in Beirut’s southern suburbs as the ceasefire came into effect — an apparent bout of spontaneous celebration.\\\".\",\"Review the highlighted misinformation or unsupported lines first, then rerun AI Fact Check after revising them.\"],\"claims\":[{\"text\":\"A 10-day ceasefire deal agreed by Lebanon and Israel took effect on April 16.\",\"subject\":\"10-day ceasefire\",\"value\":\"took effect on April 16\",\"time\":\"Apr 16\",\"match_score\":1,\"confidence\":0.9,\"source\":\"CNA\",\"reason\":\"CNA directly reports a 10-day ceasefire went into effect on Apr 16.\",\"importance\":\"core\",\"status\":\"supported\",\"claim_key\":\"9a9c2e4b3f8e\",\"draft_sentence\":\"BEIRUT – A 10-day ceasefire deal agreed by Lebanon and Israel took effect on April 16, as US President Donald Trump said he was trying to set up the first meeting between the leaders of the two countries.\",\"sentence_key\":\"65625d0b1c31\",\"sentence_index\":0},{\"text\":\"Mr Trump said the truce would begin at 5 P.M. EST (midnight local time in Lebanon and Israel / 5am Singapore).\",\"subject\":\"start time of ceasefire\",\"value\":\"5 P.M. EST / midnight local\",\"time\":\"Apr 16 (start)\",\"match_score\":0.85,\"confidence\":0.85,\"source\":\"CNA\",\"reason\":\"CNA quotes Trump saying the ceasefire would begin at 5 P.M. EST; the draft\'s local time conversion is a paraphrase.\",\"importance\":\"core\",\"status\":\"supported\",\"claim_key\":\"0f35f0e7806d\",\"draft_sentence\":\"Singapore\",\"sentence_key\":\"532585d44c19\",\"sentence_index\":10},{\"text\":\"Mr Trump said he was trying to set up the first meeting between the leaders of the two countries and expected visits to the White House over the next days.\",\"subject\":\"meeting between leaders / White House visit\",\"value\":\"Trump said he was trying to set up meeting; expected visits in next 4-5 days\",\"time\":\"within days of Apr 16\",\"match_score\":0.85,\"confidence\":0.8,\"source\":\"CNA\",\"reason\":\"CNA reports Trump saying he expected Netanyahu and Aoun to visit the White House; this is a clear paraphrase.\",\"importance\":\"core\",\"status\":\"supported\",\"claim_key\":\"42fa21df19af\",\"draft_sentence\":\"BEIRUT – A 10-day ceasefire deal agreed by Lebanon and Israel took effect on April 16, as US President Donald Trump said he was trying to set up the first meeting between the leaders of the two countries.\",\"sentence_key\":\"65625d0b1c31\",\"sentence_index\":0},{\"text\":\"The pause in hostilities was linked to US-Iran diplomacy and described as fragile.\",\"subject\":\"diplomacy / fragility\",\"value\":\"linked to US-Iran diplomacy; pause remained fragile\",\"time\":\"Apr 16\",\"match_score\":0.85,\"confidence\":0.8,\"source\":\"CNA\",\"reason\":\"CNA notes the ceasefire was linked to US-Iran talks and remained fragile.\",\"importance\":\"core\",\"status\":\"supported\",\"claim_key\":\"c57f445bf624\",\"draft_sentence\":null,\"sentence_key\":null,\"sentence_index\":null},{\"text\":\"Gunfire rang out in Beirut’s southern suburbs as the ceasefire came into effect — an apparent bout of spontaneous celebration.\",\"subject\":\"celebratory gunfire in Beirut\",\"value\":\"gunfire reported as apparent celebration\",\"time\":\"as ceasefire began Apr 16\",\"match_score\":0.2,\"confidence\":0.35,\"source\":null,\"reason\":\"No matched CNA/ST source in provided snippets confirming celebratory gunfire; treated as weakly supported by the supplied evidence.\",\"importance\":\"minor\",\"status\":\"contradicted\",\"claim_key\":\"9677c7dac5fb\",\"draft_sentence\":\"Gunfire rang out in Beirut’s southern suburbs, Hezbollah’s heartland, as the ceasefire came into effect – an apparent bout of spontaneous celebration, although that could not be confirmed.\",\"sentence_key\":\"2f3a9bfc5d4c\",\"sentence_index\":2},{\"text\":\"Israel’s military said it had struck over 380 \'Hezbollah terror organisation targets in southern Lebanon\' and was on \'high alert\' to resume strikes.\",\"subject\":\"Israeli strikes and readiness\",\"value\":\"struck over 380 targets; on high alert\",\"time\":\"as ceasefire came into force (Apr 16–18)\",\"match_score\":0.85,\"confidence\":0.8,\"source\":\"CNA\",\"reason\":\"Matched CNA coverage reports Israeli army had struck many targets and remained ready to resume strikes during the truce.\",\"importance\":\"core\",\"status\":\"supported\",\"claim_key\":\"685e6d75fcd6\",\"draft_sentence\":\"As the ceasefire came into force, Israel’s military said it had struck over 380 “Hezbollah terror organisation targets in southern Lebanon” and was on “high alert” to resume strikes.\",\"sentence_key\":\"9982eecb126c\",\"sentence_index\":5},{\"text\":\"The Middle East war began when the US and Israel attacked Iran on Feb 28, pulling Lebanon in when Hezbollah fired rockets on March 2.\",\"subject\":\"origin of war\",\"value\":\"began when US and Israel attacked Iran on Feb 28\",\"time\":\"Feb 28–Mar 2\",\"match_score\":0,\"confidence\":0.1,\"source\":null,\"reason\":\"No CNA/ST match; this core historical claim is not supported by the supplied CNA sources and contradicts normal reporting framing (no CNA/ST evidence that US and Israel attacked Iran on Feb 28).\",\"importance\":\"core\",\"status\":\"contradicted\",\"claim_key\":\"14f9b888d3ca\",\"draft_sentence\":\"The Middle East war began when the US and Israel attacked Iran on Feb 28, and Lebanon was pulled in when Hezbollah fired rockets at Israel on March 2.\",\"sentence_key\":\"cbf8bb18c1b8\",\"sentence_index\":3}],\"claim_summary\":{\"supported\":5,\"weak\":0,\"contradicted\":2,\"total\":7},\"matched_sources\":[{\"name\":\"CNA\",\"title\":\"Trump says Israel, Lebanon agree to 10-day ceasefire - CNA\",\"url\":\"https://www.channelnewsasia.com/world/trump-says-israel-lebanon-agree-10-day-ceasefire-6061491\",\"match_type\":\"direct\",\"relevance_note\":\"Directly supports the draft\'s core claim that a 10-day ceasefire was announced by Trump and was to begin on April 16, and that Trump linked the truce to talks/White House meetings.\",\"snippet\":\"US President Donald Trump said on Thursday (Apr 16) that the leaders of Israel and Lebanon have agreed to a 10-day ceasefire. \\\"These two Leaders have agreed that in order to achieve peace between their countries, they will formally begin a 10 day ceasefire at 5pm EST,\\\" he said on Truth Social.\",\"source_type\":\"article\"},{\"name\":\"CNA\",\"title\":\"Israel and Lebanon begin ceasefire - CNA\",\"url\":\"https://www.channelnewsasia.com/world/trump-lebanon-israel-10-day-ceasefire-6061491\",\"match_type\":\"direct\",\"relevance_note\":\"Directly corroborates the ceasefire taking effect on Apr 16 and links it to US-Iran diplomacy and fragile conditions, as stated in the draft.\",\"snippet\":\"A 10-day ceasefire between Lebanon and Israel went into effect on Thursday (Apr 16) and President Donald Trump said the next meeting between the United States and Iran may take place over the weekend, adding to optimism that the Iran war could be nearing an end. The pause in hostilities remained fragile.\",\"source_type\":\"article\"},{\"name\":\"CNA\",\"title\":\"Lebanon president says working on \'permanent agreements\' after Israel truce - CNA\",\"url\":\"https://www.channelnewsasia.com/world/lebanon-israel-ceasefire-aoun-trump-hezbollah-6064576\",\"match_type\":\"partial\",\"relevance_note\":\"Partially supports the draft\'s reporting of Lebanese official reaction and statements following the truce; adds context on Aoun\'s comments.\",\"snippet\":\"BEIRUT: Lebanese President Joseph Aoun said on Friday (Apr 17) that his country was on the verge of a \\\"new phase\\\" of \\\"permanent agreements\\\", after the 10-day ceasefire in the Israel-Hezbollah war went into force.\",\"source_type\":\"article\"},{\"name\":\"CNA\",\"title\":\"Israel army says established Gaza-style \'Yellow Line\' in Lebanon - CNA\",\"url\":\"https://www.channelnewsasia.com/world/israel-army-says-established-gaza-style-yellow-line-in-lebanon-6065696\",\"match_type\":\"partial\",\"relevance_note\":\"Partially supports draft claims about continued Israeli deployments and security measures (10km/‘security zone’ mention); provides evidence of Israeli operational posture during the truce.\",\"snippet\":\"The Israeli army said Saturday (Apr 18) it had established a \\\"Yellow Line\\\" in southern Lebanon... Since a ceasefire in Gaza came into effect in October, the boundary has divided the Palestinian territory into military- and Hamas-controlled zones, with Israeli troops routinely firing on anyone approaching it.\",\"source_type\":\"article\"},{\"name\":\"CNA\",\"title\":\"Israel says to use \'full force\' in Lebanon despite truce if soldiers face threat - CNA\",\"url\":\"https://www.channelnewsasia.com/world/israel-says-use-full-force-in-lebanon-despite-truce-if-soldiers-face-threat-6066466\",\"match_type\":\"partial\",\"relevance_note\":\"Supports draft assertions that Israel remained on high alert and reserved rights to resume strikes during the truce; relevant to draft\'s note about Israeli readiness to resume operations.\",\"snippet\":\"Israeli Defence Minister Israel Katz said the military had been instructed to use \\\"full force\\\" in Lebanon - even during the ongoing ceasefire - should Israeli troops face any threat. \\\"Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu and I have instructed the IDF to act with full force... including during the ceasefire, in order to protect our soldiers in Lebanon from any threat,\\\" Katz said.\",\"source_type\":\"article\"}],\"why_not_perfect\":[\"Contradicted claim(s) still lowered the score.\",\"Source quality is not at the maximum, so the evidence could still be stronger or more directly corroborated.\",\"Some context or reporting detail is still missing.\",\"Some wording is still interpretive or less neutral than ideal.\",\"The article can still be clearer in how it connects evidence to conclusions.\"],\"why_not_perfect_details\":[{\"message\":\"Source quality is not at the maximum, so the evidence could still be stronger or more directly corroborated.\",\"examples\":[{\"type\":\"source\",\"label\":\"Source example\",\"text\":\"Lebanon president says working on \'permanent agreements\' after Israel truce - CNA\",\"reason\":\"CNA - article - partial\",\"url\":\"https://www.channelnewsasia.com/world/lebanon-israel-ceasefire-aoun-trump-hezbollah-6064576\"},{\"type\":\"source\",\"label\":\"Source example\",\"text\":\"Israel army says established Gaza-style \'Yellow Line\' in Lebanon - CNA\",\"reason\":\"CNA - article - partial\",\"url\":\"https://www.channelnewsasia.com/world/israel-army-says-established-gaza-style-yellow-line-in-lebanon-6065696\"}]},{\"message\":\"Some context or reporting detail is still missing.\",\"examples\":[{\"type\":\"claim\",\"label\":\"Context to add\",\"text\":\"The Middle East war began when the US and Israel attacked Iran on Feb 28, and Lebanon was pulled in when Hezbollah fired rockets at Israel on March 2.\",\"reason\":\"No CNA/ST match; this core historical claim is not supported by the supplied CNA sources and contradicts normal reporting framing (no CNA/ST evidence that US and Israel attacked Iran on Feb 28).\",\"highlight_key\":\"cbf8bb18c1b8\",\"source\":null}]},{\"message\":\"Some wording is still interpretive or less neutral than ideal.\",\"examples\":[{\"type\":\"claim\",\"label\":\"Interpretive wording\",\"text\":\"The Middle East war began when the US and Israel attacked Iran on Feb 28, and Lebanon was pulled in when Hezbollah fired rockets at Israel on March 2.\",\"reason\":\"No CNA/ST match; this core historical claim is not supported by the supplied CNA sources and contradicts normal reporting framing (no CNA/ST evidence that US and Israel attacked Iran on Feb 28).\",\"highlight_key\":\"cbf8bb18c1b8\",\"source\":null}]},{\"message\":\"The article can still be clearer in how it connects evidence to conclusions.\",\"examples\":[{\"type\":\"claim\",\"label\":\"Clarify this sentence\",\"text\":\"Gunfire rang out in Beirut’s southern suburbs, Hezbollah’s heartland, as the ceasefire came into effect – an apparent bout of spontaneous celebration, although that could not be confirmed.\",\"reason\":\"No matched CNA/ST source in provided snippets confirming celebratory gunfire; treated as weakly supported by the supplied evidence.\",\"highlight_key\":\"2f3a9bfc5d4c\",\"source\":null},{\"type\":\"claim\",\"label\":\"Clarify this sentence\",\"text\":\"The Middle East war began when the US and Israel attacked Iran on Feb 28, and Lebanon was pulled in when Hezbollah fired rockets at Israel on March 2.\",\"reason\":\"No CNA/ST match; this core historical claim is not supported by the supplied CNA sources and contradicts normal reporting framing (no CNA/ST evidence that US and Israel attacked Iran on Feb 28).\",\"highlight_key\":\"cbf8bb18c1b8\",\"source\":null}]},{\"message\":\"Not every important claim is fully or exactly confirmed by the matched CNA/ST sources.\",\"examples\":[{\"type\":\"claim\",\"label\":\"Not fully confirmed\",\"text\":\"Gunfire rang out in Beirut’s southern suburbs, Hezbollah’s heartland, as the ceasefire came into effect – an apparent bout of spontaneous celebration, although that could not be confirmed.\",\"reason\":\"No matched CNA/ST source in provided snippets confirming celebratory gunfire; treated as weakly supported by the supplied evidence.\",\"highlight_key\":\"2f3a9bfc5d4c\",\"source\":null},{\"type\":\"claim\",\"label\":\"Not fully confirmed\",\"text\":\"The Middle East war began when the US and Israel attacked Iran on Feb 28, and Lebanon was pulled in when Hezbollah fired rockets at Israel on March 2.\",\"reason\":\"No CNA/ST match; this core historical claim is not supported by the supplied CNA sources and contradicts normal reporting framing (no CNA/ST evidence that US and Israel attacked Iran on Feb 28).\",\"highlight_key\":\"cbf8bb18c1b8\",\"source\":null}]}]}', '2026-04-28 14:33:42', 65, 3, 92, 0, 'uploads/articles/1776392761_Screenshot 2026-04-17 102303.png', 0, '2026-04-17 18:26:01', '2026-04-17 02:26:01', '2026-04-29 06:33:42', 'published', NULL, 0);
INSERT INTO `articles` (`id`, `title`, `excerpt`, `content`, `source_url`, `verification_fingerprint`, `verification_payload`, `verification_checked_at`, `author_id`, `category_id`, `trust_score`, `has_media`, `image_path`, `is_premium_only`, `published_at`, `created_at`, `updated_at`, `status`, `review_notice`, `review_notice_pending`) VALUES
(221, 'Australian PM’s ‘diesel diplomacy’ tour of S-E Asia seen as welcome bid to calm fears at home', 'Visiting Australian Prime Minister Anthony Albanese (left) with his Malaysian counterpart Anwar Ibrahim in Kuala Lumpur on April 16.', 'SYDNEY – For the past two weeks, Australian Prime Minister Anthony Albanese has travelled across South-east Asia with a simple message for regional leaders: Canberra will offer stable flows of gas and food in return for steady supplies of fuel and fertiliser.\r\n\r\nHis so-called “diesel diplomacy” tour, which covered Singapore, Brunei and Malaysia, has been well received and resulted in joint commitments to ensure energy security.\r\n\r\nBut while the visits strengthen Australia’s ties with the region, experts say the main message has been for Mr Albanese’s domestic audience.\r\n\r\n\r\nThe trips are aimed at providing reassurance that Australia, which is heavily reliant on fuel and fertiliser imports, will have steady supplies as tensions in the Middle East continue.\r\n\r\nAssociate Professor Flavio Macau, a supply chain expert at Edith Cowan University, told The Straits Times that Australia does not currently have a shortage of petrol but had a problem with panic buying, which led to hundreds of petrol stations running out of diesel or petrol.\r\n\r\nHe said Australia’s recent agreements with regional countries helped reassure farmers and others reliant on diesel and fertilisers that Asian suppliers were committed to providing steady supplies.\r\n\r\n\r\n“It helps to go to these Asian countries – it provides reassurance,” he said.\r\n\r\nTop stories\r\nExplore top stories from all sections in one place\r\n\r\nDespite the Iran war breaking out at the end of February, March saw non-oil domestic exports growing for the seventh consecutive month.\r\nBusiness\r\n\r\nSingapore key exports surge 15.3% in March on AI-driven electronics demand\r\nSmoke rises from the site of Israeli airstrikes that targeted villages on southern Lebanon on April 16.\r\nWorld\r\n\r\n10-day ceasefire deal between Israel and Lebanon takes effect\r\nSchool bus operators are hoping for more support to help them cope with surging diesel fuel costs.\r\nSingapore\r\n\r\nHigher fuel prices: MOE asks schools to consider bus fare hike requests for external activities\r\nMdm Choi Soon-duk with her 93-year-old haenyeo mentor. The Jeju haenyeo community is known for its tight-knit and matrilineal structure.\r\nAsia\r\n\r\nLife after 50: Why two women moved to Jeju and dived into a vanishing trade\r\nEmployees discuss a character from the AI-generated series \"Mahabharat\" seen on a laptop screen at Galleri5, the tech studio arm of Collective Artists Network, at its office in Bengaluru, India, November 26, 2025.\r\nAsia\r\n\r\nThe end of ‘easy’ IT jobs? India’s tech workforce faces AI challenges\r\nNetflix chief Reed Hastings will not stand for re-election at the streaming service\'s  annual meeting in June, and plans to focus on philanthropy and other pursuits.\r\nBusiness\r\n\r\nNetflix shares dive as co-founder Reed Hastings steps away\r\nInvestors have raised questions ahead of SBS Transit\'s AGM as the transport operator’s dominance in the bus segment has come under pressure.\r\nBusiness\r\n\r\nInvestors question SBS Transit’s competitiveness, board oversight ahead of AGM\r\n“This kind of trip is saying that this fuel is definitely headed to Australia and helps to provide assurance to people. Then demand goes back to normal and panic buying is less likely to happen,” he added.\r\n\r\nMr Albanese’s latest stop was Malaysia on April 16, where he met his Malaysian counterpart Anwar Ibrahim, who promised that state-owned oil and gas company Petronas will look to provide Australia with any excess fuel supplies.\r\n\r\n\r\nAustralia supplies 95 per cent of Malaysia’s imported liquefied natural gas (LNG), while Malaysia accounts for about 13 per cent of Australia’s refined fuel imports and 10 per cent of its imports of urea fertiliser.\r\n\r\n“We have obtained an assurance from Petronas that once the domestic requirements are met, there is clearly some excess,” Datuk Seri Anwar told reporters.\r\n\r\n“We have given an assurance that the priority will be to Australia... We import gas, LNG from Australia, and they have assured us of this supply,” he said.\r\n\r\n\r\nPetronas chief operating officer Mohd Jukris Abdul Wahab (third from left) with Australia’s Prime Minister Anthony Albanese (second from right) and Foreign Minister Penny Wong (third from right) at a meeting at the Petronas Twin Towers headquarters in Kuala Lumpur on April 16.\r\n\r\nPHOTO: EPA\r\n\r\nNoted Professor Nafis Alam, head of the School of Business at Monash University Malaysia: “For both countries, the visit reinforces a model of ‘strategic interdependence’, where energy, food and industrial supply chains are treated as national security priorities, positioning the bilateral relationship as a stabilising anchor in an increasingly fragmented Indo-Pacific economic landscape.”\r\n\r\nAustralia is one of the world’s three major exporters of LNG – along with the United States and Qatar – and is a major supplier in Asia, where countries have become concerned about the disruption to exports from Qatar due to the closure of the Strait of Hormuz and Iranian attacks on Qatari gas infrastructure.\r\n\r\nBut Australia relies heavily on the region for supplies of petroleum, which is used for petrol, diesel and jet fuel. More than 80 per cent of Australia’s petroleum imports come from Asia, with 29 per cent from South Korea and 20 per cent from Singapore.\r\n\r\nMr Albanese’s meetings with his counterparts in Singapore, Brunei and Malaysia have all resulted in firm declarations of a mutual commitment to cooperate on boosting regional energy security.\r\n\r\nDr Lurion De Mello, a finance lecturer at Macquarie University, was sceptical about the need for the trip, saying that Singapore, Brunei and Malaysia were already reliable suppliers.\r\n\r\n“There is no need to fly to the region to get more fuel,” he told The Straits Times. “These countries are already stable suppliers.”\r\n\r\nDuring his travels, Mr Albanese has been quick to remind the region that Australia has long been a reliable supplier of food and resources to the region.\r\n\r\nSitting alongside Mr Anwar on April 16, Mr Albanese noted that Malaysia relies on Australian farmers for almost 60 per cent of its wheat and 75 per cent of its lamb and beef.\r\n\r\nMore on this topic\r\nAustralia secures extra diesel from Brunei, South Korea to bolster war-hit supply\r\nAustralia refinery fire worsens fuel supply crunch amid Iran war\r\n“Our neighbours know that providing fuel and fertiliser to Australia helps put food on the table in this region,” he said. “We rely on each other for fuels, including crude petroleum and natural gas.”\r\n\r\nAnalysts have noted, however, that Australia has little or no capacity to offer any additional supplies of LNG as it has little scope to expand its production capacity.\r\n\r\n“I don’t think Australia can suddenly produce extra LNG. The infrastructure isn’t there. We can’t just click our fingers and have more infrastructure,” said Dr De Mello.\r\n\r\nAustralia has also been looking to ensure that it receives steady supplies of urea, even as there has been disruption to shipments from the Middle East, which typically accounts for 65 per cent of Australia’s supplies.\r\n\r\nBefore arriving in Malaysia, Mr Albanese spent two days in Brunei, becoming the first Australian leader to make a bilateral visit to the country. There was little doubt about the reason for the visit.\r\n\r\nBrunei supplies 11 per cent of Australia’s urea imports and 9 per cent of its diesel imports. Australia, for its part, is the largest trading partner of Brunei, which imports most of its food and is a large buyer of Australian meat and livestock.\r\n\r\n\r\nVisiting Australian Prime Minister Anthony Albanese (left) and Brunei\'s Sultan Hassanal Bolkiah at the Istana Nurul Iman in Bandar Seri Begawan on April 15, after signing a joint statement on energy and food security.\r\n\r\nPHOTO: REUTERS\r\n\r\nDuring his visit, Mr Albanese met Brunei’s leader, Sultan Hassanal Bolkiah, and they agreed to avoid unjustified import and export restrictions and to “consult each other on any disruptions”.\r\n\r\nThis visit followed a separate trip to Singapore, during which Mr Albanese and Singapore Prime Minister Lawrence Wong committed to make “maximum efforts to meet each other’s energy security needs in the context of the acute energy crisis currently affecting global markets”.\r\n\r\nIt was not surprising that Mr Albanese began his diesel diplomacy tour in Singapore, which is a major refined petroleum supplier but also has possibly the closest ties to Australia among the South-east Asian countries. Mr Albanese and PM Wong have met several times and refer to each other as friends.\r\n\r\n\r\nAustralian Prime Minister Albanese and Singapore Prime Minister Lawrence Wong at a joint press conference at the Istana on April 10.\r\n\r\nPHOTO: LIANHE ZAOBAO\r\n\r\nMr Albanese evidently felt that the trip was a success as he quickly returned to the region the following week.\r\n\r\nProf Macau said that while Mr Albanese’s travels were primarily to send a message to Australians, these would also help shore up Canberra’s ties with countries in the region, which would help it to withstand the current and future crises.\r\n\r\n“It is important to show that Australia is an active player in this part of the world,” he said. “Doing nothing would mean we are isolating ourselves. It is good to stretch our muscles and say we want to be part of the game.”\r\n\r\nAdditional reporting by Hazlin Hassan', 'https://www.straitstimes.com/asia/australianz/australian-pms-diesel-diplomacy-tour-of-se-asia-seen-as-welcome-bid-to-spread-calm-at-home', '952b9e7e9ca737b261dba7b1dca02c592c24d79dfc5b9aedcc68913845ad4048', '{\"fingerprint\":\"95