(231, 'Singapore Opens First Pokémon TCG Gym Deluxe at SCAPE', 'CNA Lifestyle reported that Singapore has launched its first Pokémon Trading Card Game (TCG) Gym Deluxe at *SCAPE, drawing over 150 fans on opening day. The venue offers official merchandise, rare card displays, and weekly battles, while also serving as a hub for collectors and competitive players.', 'On January 18, 2026, Singapore welcomed its first Pokémon TCG Gym Deluxe, located on the third floor of *SCAPE. The opening attracted more than 150 fans eager to purchase merchandise and participate in card battles. Licensed by The Pokémon Company, the 3,300‑square‑foot space is designed as both a retail outlet and a community arena.\r\n\r\nVisitors can shop for official Pokémon merchandise such as plushies, apparel, and collectible cards, while also learning how to play the trading card game. The venue will host weekly gym battles and monthly tournaments, creating a structured environment for competitive play. Rare cards valued at thousands of dollars are also on display, some owned by Rowell TCG founder Chong Sze Wen and other collectors.\r\n\r\nFans interviewed by CNA Lifestyle described the event as both nostalgic and exciting. Some view card collecting as a hobby, while others treat it as an investment, noting that rare Pokémon cards can appreciate faster than traditional markets. With the franchise celebrating its 30th anniversary this year, collectors expect significant shifts in card values depending on reprints and new releases.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 79, 14, 79, 0, NULL, 0, '2026-04-27 02:58:40', '2026-04-26 10:58:40', '2026-04-27 02:58:53', 'draft', 'A category expert rejected this article during final verification. Please revise it and submit it again.', 1),
(232, 'Women taking career ‘detours’ as a norm will be one indicator of work group’s success: Indranee', 'SINGAPORE - Normalising career “detours” to care for children, along with a shift in workplace culture to better align work with family life, are among the goals of a new work group set up to support', 'ComScore Observer\r\n\r\n\r\nLog in\r\n\r\nmyST+\r\n\r\nMenu\r\nWomen taking career ‘detours’ as a norm will be one indicator of work group’s success: Indranee\r\n Get ST\'s newsletters delivered to your inbox\r\n\r\nMinister in the Prime Minister’s Office Indranee Rajah said one indicator of success would be to reduce the “maternity penalty” faced by women.\r\nMinister in the Prime Minister’s Office Indranee Rajah said one indicator of success would be to reduce the “maternity penalty” faced by women.\r\n\r\nST PHOTO: GAVIN FOO\r\n\r\n\r\nGoogle Preferred Source badge\r\nPublished Apr 29, 2026, 05:50 PM\r\n\r\nUpdated Apr 29, 2026, 07:53 PM\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nListen\r\n\r\nSummarise\r\n\r\nSINGAPORE - Normalising career “detours” to care for children, along with a shift in workplace culture to better align work with family life, are among the goals of a new work group set up to support Singaporeans’ marriage and parenthood aspirations.\r\nSpeaking to the media on April 29, Minister in the Prime Minister’s Office Indranee Rajah said one indicator of success would be reducing the “maternity penalty” faced by women, and for workplaces to fully support those who choose to step away from their careers temporarily to raise children.\r\n\r\nSuch detours should not be seen as career setbacks, she said, with women feeling assured they have not let themselves or their employers down.\r\n\r\n\r\nTo do this, employers will need to actively support their return to work, including helping them catch up on developments such as new technologies and workplace tools that may have emerged during their time away.\r\n\r\nMs Indranee was giving an update on the new Marriage and Parenthood Reset Workgroup she chairs, following Singapore’s total fertility rate (TFR) falling to a historic low of 0.87 in 2025.\r\n\r\nOther members include office holders like the Ministry of Health’s Rahayu Mahzam, the Ministry of Manpower’s Dinesh Vasu Dash and the Ministry of Education’s Jasmin Lau. The work group, which was first announced during the Budget debate in February, will release a full report in early 2027.\r\n\r\n\r\nADVERTISING\r\n\r\n\r\nHousing, caregiving and pre-school education are three areas where earlier changes could be made, said Ms Indranee, adding that the work group will map out a broad, long-term strategy as part of the marriage and parenthood reset.\r\n\r\nTop stories\r\nExplore top stories from all sections in one place\r\n\r\nThe workgroup comes as the Republic hit its lowest resident total fertility rate to date at 0.87 in 2025, down from 0.97 the year before.\r\nSingapore\r\n\r\nNew workgroup to drive marriage and parenthood reset in S’pore amid record low fertility\r\nThe upgrade will see more digital technologies and robotics infused into T3, says Acting Transport Minister Jeffrey Siow. \r\nSingapore\r\n\r\nChangi Airport T3 to undergo major upgrade; tender to be called in second half of 2026\r\nPrimary 1 registration exercise at Guangyang Primary School. \r\nSingapore\r\n\r\nSmaller P1 cohort sizes for majority of primary schools from 2027 amid changing demographics\r\nPayNow\'s decision to end the nickname feature for retail customers is meant to strengthen protection against impersonation scams.\r\nBusiness\r\n\r\nNo more nicknames: PayNow to end alias option for users from June\r\nThe townhouse at 141 Neil Road is among the more than 7,200 buildings in Singapore that have been conserved thus far.\r\nSingapore\r\n\r\nDecades-old townhouse set to be S’pore’s first energy self-sufficient heritage building\r\nThe men can be seen throwing chairs at the exterior of the store and at the hoarding barrier across the eatery.\r\nSingapore\r\n\r\nTwo youths assisting police investigations for mischief over incident at 313@Somerset restaurant\r\nOn April 29, a district court approved Didier Gaspard Owen Maximilien’s application to leave the country to go to Manila for a “field experience”.\r\nSingapore\r\n\r\nTeen accused of licking iJooz straw allowed to leave Singapore for school-related trip to Manila\r\nAbout half of the issues can be addressed through policy, she said in an interview at The Treasury building.\r\n\r\n“But given that this has to do with mindsets, relationships, attitudes and, in some cases, emotions, I think we also need whole of society,” she said.\r\n\r\nBeyond individual career paths, she said a broader shift is needed in how work is structured.\r\n\r\nOne measure of success would be a workplace culture that better aligns work and family life.\r\n\r\n“When you talk to employers and employees, it’s almost as though if I have family time, somehow work has to be sacrificed. Or if I’m doing work, family has to be sacrificed. That shouldn’t be the case,” she said.\r\n\r\nShe noted that the implementation of flexible work arrangements is “patchy”, with some companies doing it well while others feel unable to adopt it, and employees feeling like such requests will be frowned upon.\r\n\r\nMore on this topic\r\nNew workgroup to drive marriage and parenthood reset in S’pore amid record low fertility\r\nBabies born to mums in their 40s on the rise amid Singapore’s fertility decline\r\nCompanies need to work out how they can reach targets and goals while not overworking employees, she said.\r\n\r\nSimple indicators of this include whether employees can leave work before dark, have dinner with their families, keep weekends largely free from work, and take holidays without receiving e-mails and messages, she added.\r\n\r\n“If it is constantly impossible for you to achieve your KPIs (key performance indicators) and still get home to have dinner, then something is wrong,” said Ms Indranee.\r\n\r\nCompanies also need better and more progressive human resources teams and work design, she said, noting that current work norms were shaped in earlier decades.\r\n\r\n\r\nWith online tools and artificial intelligence enabling greater flexibility, companies should rethink how work is organised to better support employees’ family lives, she added.\r\n\r\nMs Indranee said another key measure of the group’s success is for those who are undecided about having children to find the decision a bit easier after looking at the experiences of friends and others around them.\r\n\r\nThis means shifting perceptions away from the competition, stress and “horror stories” often associated with raising children, and towards a sense of the joy in building a family.\r\n\r\n“I’m asking and calling on all Singaporeans and organisations to work with us on this so that we can change the trajectory of our TFR,” she said.\r\n\r\nMs Nazura Huda and her husband have contemplated having children since tying the knot in 2021.\r\n\r\nBoth grew up in big families, and often bring out their cousins’ children on the weekends. But making the commitment to have their own child is not as easy, 30-year-old Ms Nazura said.\r\n\r\n“For the older generations of women in my family, it is uncommon to go to university or chase a career. Since things are different for me, I feel like I have to make the most of my prime years and hustle now,” said the marketing executive.\r\n\r\nShe is doubtful that employers will be supportive of career detours for women to raise children.\r\n\r\n“What’s in it for these companies? And even if they do let me come back after two to three years, I still would have lost out on some things, like maybe a few promotions, a more impressive portfolio of campaigns, or a bigger network built over the years,” she said.\r\n\r\nBut at the same time, building a family would still fill an emotional gap that she and her husband currently feel, she said, adding that she looks forward to seeing the workgroup’s recommendations.\r\n\r\nMore on this topic\r\nKids, in this economy? Singaporeans on the barriers to having more children, if at all\r\nSingapore wants more babies. Will a radical reset reverse the fertility crunch?\r\n\r\nSee more on\r\nIndranee Rajah\r\n\r\nChildren and youth\r\n\r\nBirth rate\r\n\r\nFertility rate\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nRECOMMENDED\r\nAd\r\nLimited-time welcome offer with HSBC Revolution Credit Card + HSBC Everyday Global Account\r\nHSBC Credit Cards\r\nAd\r\nMore rewarding relationship with HSBC Revolution Credit Card + HSBC Everyday Global Account\r\nHSBC Credit Cards\r\nAd\r\nLearn How To Write Faster for Work (Find Out Now)\r\nGrammarly\r\nAd\r\nBeyond Text Generation: An AI Tool That Helps You Write Better\r\nGrammarly\r\nTaiwanese actor Darren Wang jailed six months for illegal access to personal data\r\nKurt Tay jailed for sharing intimate video\r\nDevil Wears Prada 2 faces racism backlash in Asia over Chinese character\'s name\r\n‘If you’re overweight, don’t geh kiang’: Influencer Mayiduo injured foot after pull-up, triggering gout\r\nRECOMMENDED BUYS\r\n\r\nCustom Stickers Case\r\nCASETIFY\r\n\r\nCustom Stickers Case\r\n\r\nNEW!\r\n\r\n$99.00\r\n\r\nShop Now\r\nCD Player with Rechargeable Speaker\r\nAUGUST\r\n\r\nCD Player with Rechargeable Speaker\r\n\r\n$39.85\r\n\r\n$46.88\r\n15% OFF\r\n\r\nShop Now\r\nMoisturising Lip Balm, Magnificent Marshmallow\r\nTHE CREME SHOP\r\n\r\nMoisturising Lip Balm, Magnificent Marshmallow\r\n\r\n$13.19\r\n\r\n$18.85\r\n30% OFF\r\n\r\nShop Now\r\nDigital Food Steamer MMFS10\r\nMAYER\r\n\r\nDigital Food Steamer MMFS10\r\n\r\n$69.52\r\n\r\n$189.00\r\n63% OFF\r\n\r\nShop Now\r\nUmma: A Korean Mom\'s Kitchen Wisdom\r\nSARAH AHN & NAM SOON AHN\r\n\r\nUmma: A Korean Mom\'s Kitchen Wisdom\r\n\r\n$42.85\r\n\r\n$66.95\r\n36% OFF\r\n\r\nShop Now\r\nPlayStation 5 Slim, Digital\r\nSONY\r\n\r\nPlayStation 5 Slim, Digital\r\n\r\n$764.00\r\n\r\n$977.65\r\n22% OFF\r\n\r\nShop Now\r\nTrue Wireless Hea