(236, 'US Fed holds interest rates steady amid sharp divide over policy easing bias', 'In a shift from previous language, the Fed noted rising concerns about inflation, due in part to the recent rise in energy prices.', 'WASHINGTON - The Federal Reserve held interest rates steady on April 29, but in its most divided decision since 1992 noted rising concerns about inflation in a policy statement that drew three dissents from officials who no longer feel the US central bank should communicate a bias towards lowering borrowing costs.\r\n\r\nA fourth dissent at the meeting came in favour of a quarter-percentage-point rate cut.\r\n\r\n“Inflation is elevated, in part reflecting the recent increase in global energy prices,” the Fed said in its policy statement, a shift from previous language saying that inflation was just “somewhat” elevated.”\r\n\r\n“Developments in the Middle East are contributing to a high level of uncertainty about the economic outlook,” it said.\r\n\r\nThe 8-4 vote was the most divisive since Oct 6, 1992, and shows the breadth of opinion presumed incoming Fed Chair Kevin Warsh will face in pursuing rate cuts that President Donald Trump says he expects from his chosen successor to Mr Jerome Powell, whose term as central bank chief ends on May 15.\r\n\r\nThough the latest policy statement retained language about how the Fed would assess the “extent and timing of additional adjustments” to rates, a phrase that pointed to future cuts as the next likely move, three policymakers objected.\r\n\r\nCleveland Fed President Beth Hammack, Minneapolis Fed President Neel Kashkari and Dallas Fed President Lorie Logan, while supportive of holding the policy rate steady in the current 3.50 per cent-3.75 per cent range, “did not support inclusion of an easing bias in the statement at this time” and voted against the new statement.', 'https://www.straitstimes.com/business/us-fed-holds-interest-rates-steady-amid-sharp-divide-over-policy-easing-bias?ref=top-story', NULL, NULL, NULL, 16, 3, 99, 0, NULL, 0, '2026-04-30 06:52:50', '2026-04-30 06:52:50', '2026-04-30 06:52:50', 'published', NULL, 0),
(237, 'Suspect in Trump dinner shooting took selfie moments before attack, prosecutors say', 'An image released by the US Department of Justice on April 29 showing Cole Tomas Allen, the suspect in the Washington shooting incident appearing in a selfie picture.', 'WASHINGTON - The man accused of trying to assassinate President Donald Trump took a selfie in his hotel room moments before bursting through security with a pump-action shotgun, prosecutors said on April 29.\r\n\r\nCole Allen launched his attack just after 8.30pm on April 25, descending from his room at a Washington Hilton, then trying to get into the basement ballroom where Mr Trump and other senior officials had joined a media gala dinner, prosecutors said.\r\n\r\nThe 31-year-old was tackled and detained in a chaotic scuffle with security guards. Shots were fired but no one was killed.\r\n\r\nAccording to prosecutors, Allen spent his last minutes checking websites that covered Mr Trump’s whereabouts, arming himself, and posing for a selfie taken with a cellphone in the mirror of his room.\r\n\r\nA copy of the photo showed him dressed in black, with a red tie, and carrying a knife, a shoulder holster for a handgun and what authorities said was a bag for ammunition.\r\n\r\nAs he left his room, scheduled emails went out to friends and family with a manifesto explaining his actions.\r\n\r\nThe details of Allen’s alleged preparations for what prosecutors called an attack of “unfathomable malice” emerged in a filing asking a Washington federal court to deny bail.', 'https://www.straitstimes.com/world/united-states/suspect-in-trump-dinner-shooting-took-selfie-moments-before-attack-prosecutors-say?ref=top-story', NULL, NULL, NULL, 16, 3, 81, 0, NULL, 0, '2026-04-30 07:02:08', '2026-04-30 07:02:08', '2026-04-30 07:02:08', 'published', NULL, 0),
(238, 'Austria falling short in fight against money laundering, says global watchdog', 'A general view of the city skyline in Vienna, Austria, April 16, 2025. REUTERS/Elisabeth Mandl', 'VIENNA, April 30 - Austria continues to fall short in its investigation and prosecution of money laundering despite facing a range of illicit finance threats as a gateway to Eastern Europe, the global financial crime watchdog said in a report on Thursday.\r\n\r\nThe Paris-based Financial Action Task Force found in the so-called mutual evaluation report, a review carried out by experts from other FATF countries, that while Austria had made some progress since its last such review 10 years ago, much still needed to be done.\r\n\r\n\"While Austria has improved its understanding and assessment of money laundering and terrorist financing risks since its last mutual evaluation, a nuanced and consistent risk understanding across all competent authorities is yet to be achieved,\" the FATF, an intergovernmental body, said in a statement.\r\n\r\nWeaknesses identified included \"insufficient resources as well as limitations in operational independence\" at the Austrian Financial Intelligence Unit, a unit of the Criminal Intelligence Service that deals with money laundering, the FATF said.\r\n\r\nThe punishment imposed for money laundering offences in Austria is also not effective in dissuading would-be offenders, the watchdog said, adding that such sanctions had not yet been applied against any legal person, such as a company.\r\n\r\n\"Restrictive interpretations of the money laundering offence and high standards of proof continue to limit investigation and prosecution,\" the FATF said, adding that the number of money laundering convictions remains low.\r\n\r\nThere are also too few investigators and prosecutors to ensure the recovery of assets linked to financial crimes, and the level of seized assets returned to victims is low, it said.', 'https://www.straitstimes.com/world/europe/austria-falling-short-in-fight-against-money-laundering-says-global-watchdog?ref=latest-headlines', NULL, NULL, NULL, 16, 3, 94, 0, 'uploads/articles/1777504190_d5d0beaa3f16165685deab30314ee636524f14da865cc32cfcc74e199a9eacf5.webp', 0, '2026-04-30 07:09:50', '2026-04-30 07:09:50', '2026-04-30 07:09:50', 'published', NULL, 0),
(239, 'Playstation 5 rising prices', 'PlayStation 5 prices could rise in Singapore amid Sony’s South-east Asia adjustments', 'SINGAPORE – Buying a PlayStation 5 (PS5) in Singapore could cost up to around $100 more from May.\r\n\r\nIts Japanese maker Sony has updated its recommended retail prices for the gaming consoles in South-east Asia amid global economic pressures.\r\n\r\nAccording to a April 27 update on the official PlayStation Blog, prices for the three versions of the console are set to rise on May 1 by around $50 to $100:\r\n\r\n\r\nPS5: $849 (formerly $799)\r\n\r\nPS5 digital edition: $764 ($669)\r\n\r\nPS5 Pro: $1,167 ($1,069)\r\n\r\nWhile Sony has listed recommended retail prices for the popular console, both authorised and unauthorised e-commerce retailers in Singapore have sold the PS5 at lower prices, especially during fairs like the IT Show.\r\n\r\nOnline checks on Singapore retailers on April 27, including Courts, Gain City and Harvey Norman as well as Orchard Road-based Qisahn, show the consoles mostly in stock retailing at their pre-adjusted recommended retail prices.\r\n\r\n“With continued pressures in the global economic landscape, we’ve made the decision to increase the prices of PS5, PS5 Pro, and PlayStation Portal remote player globally,” said Sony Interactive Entertainment’s vice-president for global marketing Isabelle Tomatis in the blog post.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nThe post was first published a month earlier for price changes affecting the US, Britain and European regions, as well as its home in Japan. Price changes in those regions kicked in on April 2.\r\n\r\nTop stories\r\nExplore top stories from all sections in one place\r\n\r\nPrime Minister Lawrence Wong earlier unveiled a record $154.7 billion Budget to provide more support for firms to stay competitive and make a national push to tap the potential of artificial intelligence (AI). \r\nSingapore\r\n\r\nPM Wong to speak at May Day Rally on helping workers adapt to evolving roles due to AI, new tech\r\nThe new rules are meant to give travellers greater convenience.\r\nSingapore\r\n\r\nCross-border taxis can drop off passengers anywhere in S\'pore, parts of Johor from May 4\r\nBrent crude, the global oil benchmark, surged 5.4 per cent to US$124.40 a barrel as at 12pm Singapore time, after jumping more than 6 per cent the previous day.\r\nBusiness\r\n\r\nOil hit new war high on report Trump to be briefed on potential Iran strikes\r\nDuring the collaboration, officers raided 382 locations in Singapore, Hong Kong, Japan, Malaysia, South Korea, Thailand and Brunei.\r\nSingapore\r\n\r\nOver 400 arrested, investigated in multi-national operation against online child sexual exploitation\r\n(From left) Ms Siti Amirah Mohamed Asrori, Ms Annamalai Kokila Parvathi and Ms Mossammad Sobikun Nahar arriving at Supreme Court on April 30\r\nSingapore\r\n\r\nJudge overturns acquittal of three women who organised procession outside Istana, each fined $3k\r\n\r\nSingapore\r\n\r\nWhy young Singaporeans see having kids as a loss, instead of something to be gained\r\nForeigners and permanent residents without MyKad are still allowed to buy unsubsidised RON95 petrol if they are using locally-registered vehicles.\r\nAsia\r\n\r\n‘Vehicle plate, not nationality’: Official says foreigners can still pump RON95\r\nWhile this may be good news for port operators, it could spell other risks for the global economy such as disrupting the flow of trade.\r\nBusiness\r\n\r\nShorter waits at Asia ports, including Singapore, as ships use ‘slow steaming’ to cut fuel cost\r\nShe added that the price change was “a necessary step to ensure we can continue delivering innovative, high-quality gaming experiences to players worldwide”.\r\n\r\nOther South-east Asian countries including Malaysia, Thailand, Indonesia, the Philippines and Vietnam will also have new suggested retail prices set by Sony starting May 1.\r\n\r\n\r\nEarly reactions to the blog post announcing the impending price hike have not been positive. One gamer on X called it “pure greed, to increase the price without giving back anything” for a gaming console first released in November 2020.\r\n\r\nSales for the console had been on the decline. Sony sold around 8.1 million PS5 units in the three months ending Dec 31, 2025, marking a drop from the 9.5 million units sold during the same period in 2024.', 'https://www.straitstimes.com/singapore/playstation-5-prices-could-rise-in-singapore-amid-sonys-south-east-asia-adjustments?ref=search-results', 'a2f8a793934443c01fac6695b698da8cb23c24028fad4adec4b7d688d8d3cc45', '{\"fingerprint\":\"a2f8a793934443c01fac6695b698da8cb23c24028fad4adec4b7d688d8d3cc45\",\"trust_score\":87,\"passed\":true,\"publish_decision\":\"auto_publish\",\"summary\":\"Only one trusted source strongly supports the draft, so confidence remains limited. The supplied reference URL appears to be a valid exact CNA/ST article link.\",\"verdict\":\"Reliable. Auto publish approved because the CNA/ST evidence is strong enough for direct publication.\",\"metrics\":{\"factual_accuracy\":84,\"source_quality\":92,\"bias_detection\":80,\"logical_consistency\":80,\"completeness\":70},\"rubric_metrics\":{\"factual_accuracy\":38,\"source_quality\":23,\"bias_detection\":8,\"logical_consistency\":8,\"completeness\":7},\"source_url\":\"https://www.straitstimes.com/singapore/playstation-5-prices-could-rise-in-singapore-amid-sonys-south-east-asia-adjustments?ref=search-results\",\"source_label\":\"Compared against 2 trusted CNA/ST sources.\",\"misinformation_highlights\":[],\"improvement_suggestions\":[\"Add missing context such as who said what, when it happened, where it happened, and what was officially announced.\"],\"claims\":[{\"text\":\"Buying a PlayStation 5 (PS5) in Singapore could cost up to around $100 more from May.\",\"subject\":\"PlayStation 5 price in Singapore\",\"value\":\"up to around $100 increase\",\"time\":\"from May 1, 2026\",\"match_score\":1,\"confidence\":0.95,\"source\":\"ST\",\"r