the same price figures and May 1 effective date cited in the draft; it directly supports the draft\'s core claims.\",\"snippet\":\"SINGAPORE - Buying a PlayStation 5 (PS5) in Singapore could cost up to around $100 more from May. ... According to a April 27 update on the official PlayStation Blog, prices for the three versions of the console are set to rise on May 1 by around $50-$100: PS5: $849 (formerly $799) PS5 digital edition: $764 ($669) PS5 Pro: $1,167 ($1,069).\",\"source_type\":\"article\"},{\"name\":\"CNA\",\"title\":\"Sony announces PS5 price hikes in some regions\",\"url\":\"https://www.straitstimes.com/tech/sony-announces-ps5-price-hikes-in-some-regions\",\"match_type\":\"related\",\"relevance_note\":\"Provides background and precedent on Sony\'s earlier regional price adjustments and the company\'s stated reasons; useful context but not the specific Apr 27 South-east Asia update.\",\"snippet\":\"TOKYO - Japan’s Sony said it has hiked the price of some PlayStation 5 consoles in select markets, but not the United States, because of \'challenging\' global economic conditions. The company said on April 13 in a blog post the \'tough decision\' had been taken \'with a backdrop of a challenging economic environment, including high inflation and fluctuating exchange rates\'.\",\"source_type\":\"article\"}],\"why_not_perfect\":[\"Source quality is not at the maximum, so the evidence could still be stronger or more directly corroborated.\",\"Some context or reporting detail is still missing.\",\"Some wording is still interpretive or less neutral than ideal.\",\"The article can still be clearer in how it connects evidence to conclusions.\",\"Not every important claim is fully or exactly confirmed by the matched CNA/ST sources.\"],\"why_not_perfect_details\":[{\"message\":\"Source quality is not at the maximum, so the evidence could still be stronger or more directly corroborated.\",\"examples\":[{\"type\":\"source\",\"label\":\"Source example\",\"text\":\"Sony announces PS5 price hikes in some regions\",\"reason\":\"CNA - article - related\",\"url\":\"https://www.straitstimes.com/tech/sony-announces-ps5-price-hikes-in-some-regions\"}]},{\"message\":\"Some context or reporting detail is still missing.\",\"examples\":[]},{\"message\":\"Some wording is still interpretive or less neutral than ideal.\",\"examples\":[]},{\"message\":\"The article can still be clearer in how it connects evidence to conclusions.\",\"examples\":[]},{\"message\":\"Not every important claim is fully or exactly confirmed by the matched CNA/ST sources.\",\"examples\":[]}]}', '2026-04-30 16:55:53', 70, 14, 87, 0, NULL, 0, '2026-04-30 16:56:00', '2026-04-30 16:50:57', '2026-04-30 16:56:00', 'published', NULL, 0),
(240, 'Singapore to Host Game On, Dreamers! PlayFest and Evo 2026', 'Singapore will stage its largest homegrown gaming festival, Game On, Dreamers! PlayFest, in August 2026, featuring esports, cosplay, and immersive experiences. In addition, the country will host Evo 2', 'The Straits Times reported that Singapore’s gaming calendar for 2026 will be highlighted by two landmark events. The first is Game On, Dreamers! PlayFest, scheduled for August 7–9 at the Sands Expo & Convention Centre. Branded as “G.O.D. Mode,” the festival is designed “for gamers, by gamers,” and will bring together esports competitions, cosplay showcases, indie creators, and immersive gaming zones. With strategic partnerships from CAPCOM and Social Seed, the event promises a mix of international content and local engagement. Supported by the Singapore Tourism Board, PlayFest is expected to strengthen Singapore’s appeal as a destination for gaming culture and leisure.\r\n\r\nThe second major event is the Evolution Championship Series (Evo 2026), which will be hosted in Singapore for the first time. Evo is widely regarded as the “World Cup” of fighting games, featuring iconic franchises such as Street Fighter, Tekken, King of Fighters, and Mortal Kombat. The tournament will follow its traditional double‑elimination format and is expected to attract thousands of competitors from around the world. Prize pools are projected to exceed US$30,000 per title, and Singaporean pro gamer Ho Kun Xian, who won Evo 2013, has already confirmed his participation.\r\n\r\nTogether, these events highlight Singapore’s dual focus on community‑driven gaming festivals and elite esports tournaments. Industry observers note that this combination positions Singapore as a leading hub for gaming in Asia, blending grassroots engagement with global prestige.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 21, 14, 77, 0, NULL, 0, '2026-05-02 22:43:29', '2026-04-30 17:03:36', '2026-05-02 22:43:29', 'published', NULL, 0),
(241, 'Singapore Schools to Pilot AI Tools in Classrooms', 'The Straits Times reported that primary and secondary schools in Singapore will begin piloting AI‑powered tools in 2026 to support personalized learning.', 'According to The Straits Times, the Ministry of Education (MOE) is launching a pilot program that introduces AI‑powered learning assistants into classrooms. These tools will analyze student performance data and provide teachers with insights on areas where students may need additional support. The goal is to make lessons more adaptive, allowing educators to adjust pacing and content for different learning profiles.\r\n\r\nOfficials emphasized that AI will not replace teachers but serve as a complementary tool. Teachers will remain central to lesson delivery, while AI systems provide real‑time feedback and recommendations. Safeguards are being put in place to ensure that student data is handled responsibly, with strict privacy and security protocols.\r\n\r\nThe Straits Times noted that the pilot reflects Singapore’s broader push to integrate technology into education. By experimenting with AI in classrooms, MOE hopes to enhance learning outcomes, reduce administrative burdens on teachers, and prepare students for a future where digital literacy is essential.\r\n\r\nParents and educators have expressed cautious optimism, welcoming the potential benefits but stressing the importance of balancing technology with human interaction. Analysts suggest that if successful, the pilot could pave the way for wider adoption of AI in Singapore’s education system.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 74, 20, 80, 0, NULL, 0, '2026-04-30 17:36:43', '2026-04-30 17:36:43', '2026-04-30 17:36:43', 'pending', NULL, 0),
(242, 'New tripartite council to help workers keep jobs, companies transform amid AI disruption', 'SINGAPORE – A new tripartite council will be set up to double down on plans to upskill workers and help companies redesign jobs and adopt new tools, amid a disruption driven by artificial intelligence', 'New tripartite council to help workers keep jobs, companies transform amid AI disruption\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nNew tripartite council to help workers keep jobs, companies transform amid AI disruption\r\n Get ST\'s newsletters delivered to your inbox\r\n\r\nCMG20260429-KwongKC02 /邝启聪/郑智浩/NTUC, MOM, SNEF to Address AI\'s Impact on Workers, Jobs and Business / New Tripartite Jobs Council to help workers upskill, support business transformation, and redesign jobs amid AI disruption /L to R/ Ng Chee Meng, Secretary-General, NTUC , Tan See Leng, Minister for Manpower, Tan Hee Teck, President, SNEF/NTUC HQ【embargoed 30/4, 6pm】\r\n(From left) NTUC secretary-general Ng Chee Meng, Manpower Minister Tan See Leng and SNEF president Tan Hee Teck will co-chair the new Tripartite Jobs Council.\r\n\r\nPHOTO: LIANHE ZAOBAO\r\n\r\n\r\nGoogle Preferred Source badge\r\nPublished Apr 30, 2026, 05:00 PM\r\n\r\nUpdated Apr 30, 2026, 05:00 PM\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nListen\r\nSINGAPORE – A new tripartite council will be set up to double down on plans to upskill workers and help companies redesign jobs and adopt new tools, amid a disruption driven by artificial intelligence.\r\n\r\nThe Tripartite Jobs Council will comprise workers trade union National Trades Union Congress (NTUC), the Ministry of Manpower (MOM), and employers trade union Singapore National Employers Federation (SNEF).\r\n\r\nWhile AI offers productivity boosts and new job opportunities, a major concern is the reshaping of skills as AI automates many job functions including writing and coding.\r\n\r\n\r\n“We recognise that there are many workers and businesses that are both anxious and uncertain about what lies ahead,” said Minister for Manpower Tan See Leng during a press event on April 29 held at NTUC Centre.\r\n\r\n“There are valid concerns about job security, the relevance of skills, worries about obsolescence, as well as the rapid, accelerating pace of change. These are real concerns, and we take them very seriously.”\r\n\r\nDr Tan will co-chair the new Tripartite Jobs Council with NTUC secretary-general Ng Chee Meng and SNEF president Tan Hee Teck. More members and detailed plans will be announced in the coming months.\r\n\r\nOverall, the new council aims to build on existing programmes that have been set up by the tripartite partners, coordinate efforts as a centralised node, and deepen their reach to more workers and companies in every sector.\r\n\r\nTop stories\r\nExplore top stories from all sections in one place\r\n\r\nThere are 21 AI tools eligible for subsidies under this programme, including ChatGPT, Claude, Github Copilot and Midjourney.\r\nSingapore\r\n\r\nNTUC members to get subsidies on 21 AI tools from May, part of efforts to equip workers\r\nManpower Minister Tan See Leng highlighted improvements in workers’ livelihoods, as real incomes for the median worker grew by 1.6 per cent per year over the past five years\r\nBusiness\r\n\r\nSingapore must equip workers for AI-driven future: MOM, NTUC, SNEF\r\nThe new rules are meant to give travellers greater convenience.\r\nSingapore\r\n\r\nCross-border taxis can drop off passengers anywhere in S\'pore, parts of Johor from May 4\r\nThe total rainfall for the first fortnight of May is forecast to be above average over most parts of the island.\r\nSingapore\r\n\r\nThundery showers, warm weather expected in first half of May\r\nDuring the collaboration, officers raided 382 locations in Singapore, Hong Kong, Japan, Malaysia, South Korea, Thailand and Brunei.\r\nSingapore\r\n\r\nOver 400 arrested, investigated in multi-national operation against online child sexual exploitation\r\n(From left) Ms Siti Amirah Mohamed Asrori, Ms Annamalai Kokila Parvathi and Ms Mossammad Sobikun Nahar arriving at Supreme Court on April 30\r\nSingapore\r\n\r\nJudge overturns acquittal of three women who organised procession outside Istana, each fined $3k\r\n\r\nSingapore\r\n\r\nWhy young Singaporeans see having kids as a loss, instead of something to be gained\r\n“By harnessing the combined reach and capabilities of our unions, employers and the Government, we will help every worker maximise their potential, and every business can thrive by bringing out the best in their people,” he said.\r\n\r\nThe council will also complement efforts under the National AI Council, an inter-ministerial council chaired by Prime Minister Lawrence Wong that will drive AI missions in the four key sectors of advanced manufacturing, transport connectivity, finance, and healthcare.\r\n\r\nSpecifically, the council will focus on providing:\r\n\r\nCoordinated support for enterprise workforce transformation in tandem with AI adoption\r\n\r\nBroad-based and sectoral training for workers, as well as targeted transition support for at-risk worker segments and occupations to facilitate timely transitions\r\n\r\nSupport in generating public awareness of AI so more will go for training\r\n\r\nDr Tan, who is also Minister-in-charge of Energy and Science and Technology, cited results of an unreleased MOM survey done between January and March which found that 70 per cent of 2,560 companies in Singapore have not adopted AI for work.\r\n\r\nEarlier in February, the Government announced a suite of AI initiatives that would be rolled out progressively in 2026. Among them is an initiative that would give Singaporeans who take up selected SkillsFuture AI courses six months of free access to premium AI tools. Details of the qualifying courses and tools will be released later in 2026.\r\n\r\nPlans are also in the works to train some 100,000 “AI bilingual” workers by 2029, by equipping the workforce with AI know-how to solve problems and redesign work processes for more efficiency. For a start, AI fluency programmes will be crafted for the accountancy and legal professions.\r\n\r\nLater in 2026, a self-diagnostic tool developed by SkillsFuture Singapore to help workers assess their AI skill level will also be rolled out online.\r\n\r\nStarting in May, NTUC members will be able to receive up to 50 per cent off subscription costs for a suite of more than 20 eligible AI tools that can support tasks like content creation and writing, by tapping their Union Training Assistance Programme benefits.\r\n\r\nMore on this topic\r\nSingapore adds more jobs in Q1 of 2026 but fewer firms planning to hire, raise salaries\r\nPM Wong to speak at May Day Rally on helping workers adapt to evolving roles due to AI, new tech\r\nThis comes under the AI-Ready SG initiative that was launched in February, which also offers AI training pathways that refer to a set of curricula catered to workers’ skill level, job, and sector. More than 4,000 workers have enrolled for AI training courses since this initiative was launched, said NTUC on April 30.\r\n\r\nThe labour chief, Mr Ng, acknowledged concerns among young people that skills and academic qualifications can no longer guarantee their first job, and worries among seniors about job security.\r\n\r\nDr Tan said matching young people to available jobs will be one of the council’s key focuses. He added that this includes fresh graduates from the institutes of higher learningInstitutes of Higher Learning, polytechnics, Institute of Technical Education, and private educational institutions.\r\n\r\n“(We will) work closely with e2i under the labour movement, and with SNEF.”\r\n\r\nAmong the companies that would require more help are small and medium-sized enterprises (SMEs), said SNEF’s Mr Tan, who added that they face practical challenges such as cost of adopting AI tools, skills gap, and lack of in-house expertise.\r\n\r\nThough adoption of AI among SMEs rose to 14.5 per cent in 2024 from 4.2 per cent in 2023, this number still lags far behind the adoption rate of more than 60 per cent in larger companies, said Mr Tan, citing statistics from the Singapore Digital Economy Report 2025.\r\n\r\n“If this trend continues, the divide will widen not just between SMEs and larger firms, but between workers as well.”\r\n\r\nThough there are growing concerns about companies laying off staff and reducing hiring due to AI adoption, Dr Tan said there is “very little evidence of widespread displacement”.\r\n\r\nCiting results of the unreleased MOM study, he said that 6.2 per cent of companies reported that they had reduced headcount.\r\n\r\n“About 18.9 per cent of them are redesigning roles and creating new AI-related jobs,” said Dr Tan, who added that this shows that AI is primarily transforming tasks, rather than replacing jobs or workers.\r\n\r\nEarlier in February, PM Wong assured Singaporeans that the country will not have jobless growth amid the national push for AI to be more embedded in its economy.\r\n\r\n“We will exploit AI to grow the economy, and we will ensure that growth translates into good jobs and better wages,” said PM Wong in his wrap-up Budget speech on Feb 26.\r\n\r\nMore on this topic\r\nSingapore to monitor employment, wage growth as it rolls out AI programme: Josephine Teo\r\nSingapore’s national AI push: What does it mean for businesses, workers?\r\nSarah Koh is a journalist on the technology beat at The Straits Times. She takes an interest in the development of consumer tech and the impact of AI on society.\r\n\r\nFull individual access, on any device\r\n\r\nFrom morning headlines to evening reads, enjoy unlimited access.\r\n\r\nST One Digital Plan\r\nMonthly Recurring\r\n$9.90/month\r\nUnlock premium\r\nNo lock-in contract\r\nEnjoy these subscriber benefits\r\n\r\nAccess all subscriber-only content on ST app and straitstimes.com\r\nDigital access for one reader\r\nE-paper with 2-week archive so you won’t miss out on content that matters to you\r\nSee more o